const __vite_ssr_export_default__ = /* #__PURE__ */ JSON.parse("[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-01\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Artificial Intelligence in Education and Human Teachers\",\n    \"promptText\": \"With rapid advancements in artificial intelligence and automated tutoring algorithms, some people believe that human classroom teachers will eventually become obsolete.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis statement\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge AI's personalized learning prowess, but strongly disagree that human teachers will become obsolete.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: What AI can do\",\n        \"focus\": \"Automated grading, adaptive pace\",\n        \"tips\": \"AI excels at data crunching, syntax drills, and diagnosing knowledge gaps without fatigue.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Irreplaceable human role\",\n        \"focus\": \"Emotional intelligence, ethics & mentorship\",\n        \"tips\": \"Teachers inspire curiosity, nurture empathy, provide pastoral care, and teach moral reasoning.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesize & restate stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"AI will serve as a powerful teaching assistant, augmenting rather than supplanting human educators.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The meteoric proliferation of artificial intelligence in educational technology has ignited vigorous debates over the future of the teaching profession. While automated algorithms possess extraordinary capacities to deliver bespoke instructional content, I completely disagree with the assertion that human teachers will become obsolete. Rather, algorithmic platforms will serve as collaborative pedagogical tools, while human educators remain irreplaceable mentors.\\n\\nUndoubtedly, artificial intelligence offers compelling technical advantages in modern pedagogy. Machine-learning algorithms can dynamically analyze student performance data in real time, pinpointing specific conceptual deficiencies and customizing practice exercises to suit individual learning velocities. Furthermore, automated software can handle labor-intensive bureaucratic tasks—such as grading multiple-choice assessments and scheduling coursework—thereby freeing up academic staff. In resource-constrained developing regions, mobile AI tutors can also democratize access to high-quality language drills and mathematics instruction for disadvantaged learners who lack local school infrastructure.\\n\\nNevertheless, teaching is fundamentally an empathetic social vocation rather than a purely transactional dissemination of facts. Human educators instill critical non-cognitive competencies that machines cannot replicate, including emotional resilience, ethical judgment, collaborative conflict resolution, and intellectual curiosity. A skilled teacher recognizes when a student is disengaged due to domestic distress, offering psychological encouragement and tailored pastoral care that an algorithmic script is incapable of providing. Moreover, teachers serve as moral role models who challenge adolescents to question systemic biases, conduct philosophical debates, and develop nuanced interpersonal empathy.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although artificial intelligence will undoubtedly transform instructional delivery by handling individualized factual drilling, it cannot substitute for the emotional intelligence, ethical guidance, and mentorship provided by human teachers. The future classroom will be defined by human-AI symbiosis, not the erasure of the educator.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Response: Clear, nuanced, unwavering stance developed with depth throughout. Coherence: Masterful paragraph transitions ('Undoubtedly', 'Nevertheless', 'In conclusion'). Lexical Resource: Sophisticated academic vocabulary ('meteoric proliferation', 'bespoke instructional content', 'human-AI symbiosis'). Grammar: Variety of complex and conditional structures.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear thesis statement in introduction\",\n      \"Strong topic sentences for both body paragraphs\",\n      \"Over 250 words\",\n      \"Restated thesis in conclusion without introducing new points\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-02\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Individual Responsibility vs Government Action on Climate Change\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that individual lifestyle changes are the most effective way to combat global climate change. Others argue that significant environmental protection can only be achieved through government legislation and corporate regulation.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & state opinion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Both individual choices and macro policy matter, but government mandates are indispensable for systemic change.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Individual actions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Consumer demand & cultural norms\",\n        \"tips\": \"Plant-based diets, public transit, solar panels, boycotting polluting brands create consumer momentum.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Government & corporate regulation\",\n        \"focus\": \"Systemic scale & enforcement\",\n        \"tips\": \"Carbon taxes, banning fossil fuel subsidies, mandating renewable grid standards, regulating industrial giants.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Balanced synthesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Grassroots habits spark awareness, but top-down legislative frameworks yield the decisive emissions cuts required.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The intensifying planetary climate crisis has stimulated divergent viewpoints regarding where the primary locus of environmental responsibility resides. While proponents of grassroots activism emphasize the cumulative power of individual consumer choices, I side with those who argue that meaningful ecological remediation is unattainable without binding state legislation and stringent corporate regulation.\\n\\nOn the one hand, advocates of individual accountability contend that consumer habits directly shape market incentives. When millions of households voluntarily adopt plant-based diets, install rooftop photovoltaic panels, or commute via public transit rather than single-occupancy vehicles, aggregate greenhouse gas emissions decrease measurably. Furthermore, widespread consumer boycotts of single-use plastics and environmentally negligent corporations compel manufacturing brands to re-evaluate their supply chains. Individual lifestyle modifications also foster a heightened civic eco-consciousness, creating a cultural milieu wherein sustainable practices become normalized social expectations.\\n\\nOn the other hand, the sheer scale of global emissions renders voluntary consumerism insufficient on its own. The vast majority of carbon emissions are generated by heavy industry, commercial shipping, aviation, and fossil fuel power generation—sectors fundamentally insulated from individual grocery shopping choices. Only sovereign governments possess the regulatory authority to enact carbon pricing mechanisms, eliminate subsidies for oil exploration, and mandate transition timelines to renewable electric grids. For instance, the European Union's emissions trading system has achieved substantial reductions in industrial carbon output precisely because non-compliance incurs severe financial penalties that corporate boardrooms cannot ignore.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although individual consumer discipline is praiseworthy and culturally vital, it represents only a fraction of the climate equation. Because voluntary lifestyle changes lack enforcement mechanisms against multinational polluters, aggressive government legislation and statutory industrial regulations remain the paramount vehicle for ecological preservation.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Balanced discussion of both sides with clear personal perspective woven throughout. High-level academic lexicon ('locus of environmental responsibility', 'remediation', 'milieu', 'sovereign governments').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explored both perspectives fairly\",\n      \"Clear personal opinion stated in intro and conclusion\",\n      \"Distinct supporting examples cited (EU emissions trading)\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-03\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Urban Overcrowding and Housing Affordability\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many major cities around the world, housing has become increasingly unaffordable, forcing lower-income families to live in substandard conditions or commute long distances.\\n\\nWhat are the primary causes of this problem, and what practical solutions can governments implement to address it?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase issue\",\n        \"tips\": \"Housing affordability crisis in global metropolises; acknowledge dual causes and propose targeted policy solutions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Primary causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Speculative investment & supply constriction\",\n        \"tips\": \"Institutional investors treating homes as financial assets; restrictive municipal zoning preventing dense construction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Practical solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Public social housing & zoning reform\",\n        \"tips\": \"Construct state-subsidized social housing, reform zoning for mid-rise density near transit, tax vacant properties.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of actions\",\n        \"tips\": \"Rebalance housing from speculative commodity to essential human right through statutory planning.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"In recent decades, escalating real estate prices across global metropolitan centers have created an acute housing crisis, marginalizing working-class households and exacerbating socioeconomic inequality. This predicament is driven principally by the financialization of residential property and restrictive municipal zoning laws, but it can be substantially mitigated through robust social housing investment and tax reforms targeting vacant dwellings.\\n\\nThe genesis of urban unaffordability stems from two intersecting structural dynamics. Foremost is the transformation of residential housing from a social necessity into a speculative global financial asset. Private equity funds, institutional investors, and affluent overseas buyers purchase residential units not for habitation, but as capital preservation vehicles, inflating property valuations far beyond median wage growth. Concurrently, outdated municipal planning regulations and NIMBYism (not-in-my-backyard resistance) restrict the supply of new homes by prohibiting medium- and high-density residential developments near transport hubs, creating severe artificial scarcity.\\n\\nTo remediate this crisis, municipal authorities must enact decisive statutory interventions. First, governments should invest directly in the large-scale construction of high-quality, state-owned social housing, modeled on successful systems such as Singapore's Housing & Development Board or Vienna's municipal housing cooperatives. By providing long-term, rent-capped tenancies to low- and middle-income families, the state establishes a non-market price ceiling that stabilizes the private rental sector. Additionally, policymakers should levy punitive taxation on vacant luxury properties and multiple-home ownership to curb speculative hoarding, while rezoning suburban transport corridors for mixed-use mid-rise development.\\n\\nIn conclusion, urban housing unaffordability is caused by untamed financial speculation combined with artificial planning bottlenecks. By expanding municipal public housing reserves and penalizing property hoarding, governments can ensure that cities remain livable and inclusive for all socioeconomic classes.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Directly identifies causes (financialization, restrictive zoning) and concrete solutions (Singapore/Vienna model, vacancy tax). Outstanding lexical precision.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear cause-and-solution structure\",\n      \"Concrete real-world examples (Vienna/Singapore)\",\n      \"Strong cohesive progression\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-04\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Cashless Societies and Digital Transactions\",\n    \"promptText\": \"An increasing number of nations are transitioning away from physical paper currency towards entirely cashless, digital payment economies.\\n\\nDo the advantages of a cashless society outweigh its disadvantages?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase trend & state thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Digital banking offers convenience and anti-crime benefits, but severe risks to privacy, elderly exclusion, and power outages mean disadvantages outweigh advantages unless safeguarded.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Efficiency, anti-tax evasion, convenience\",\n        \"tips\": \"Seamless electronic payments, reduces street muggings, curbs black market and unrecorded cash transactions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Disadvantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Digital exclusion, surveillance, grid fragility\",\n        \"tips\": \"Marginalization of elderly/unbanked citizens, complete loss of financial privacy, vulnerability to blackouts and cyberattacks.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesize & conclude\",\n        \"tips\": \"While cashless payments should be expanded, retaining physical cash as a resilient backstop remains crucial.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The rapid acceleration of electronic commerce and mobile banking apps has prompted several advanced economies to contemplate the complete eradication of physical banknotes and coins. While transitioning to an exclusively digital payment ecosystem delivers undeniable logistical efficiencies and curtails black-market tax evasion, I believe that the attendant disadvantages—namely financial exclusion, surveillance risks, and infrastructural vulnerability—are far more profound.\\n\\nOn the positive side, digital transactions streamline commerce and enhance economic transparency. Eliminating physical currency dramatically reduces cash-handling overheads for businesses, accelerates retail transactions, and eliminates street muggings targeting paper cash. More significantly for state treasuries, electronic ledgers create immutable audit trails, making it exceptionally arduous for enterprises to evade corporate taxes or facilitate illicit laundering through informal cash-in-hand transactions. For everyday consumers, the sheer convenience of tapping a smartphone or payment card eliminates the friction of carrying bulky wallets and visiting automatic teller machines.\\n\\nConversely, the perils of an exclusively cashless system are severe and frequently overlooked. Primarily, it disenfranchises the most vulnerable strata of society: elderly citizens who struggle with digital interfaces, unbanked migrant laborers, and homeless individuals who depend on donated coins for sustenance. Furthermore, when every financial exchange is mediated by centralized corporate payment gateways, citizens forfeit all financial privacy; financial institutions and state authorities gain the power to monitor, monetize, or freeze individual purchasing behaviors at will. Finally, digital economies possess catastrophic structural fragility: during severe power blackouts, natural disasters, or cyberwarfare attacks that disable telecommunication networks, an entire population could find itself instantly incapable of buying emergency food or medicine.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although digital payments offer unprecedented commercial convenience and fiscal transparency, the catastrophic risks of digital exclusion, systemic cyber vulnerability, and total loss of privacy demonstrate that the disadvantages of a wholly cashless society outweigh its benefits. Physical currency must be preserved as an essential civil backstop.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Clear evaluation of the 'outweigh' prompt. Body paragraphs explore each side systematically with sophisticated vocabulary ('immutable audit trails', 'disenfranchises', 'systemic cyber vulnerability').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear answer to 'do advantages outweigh disadvantages'\",\n      \"Balanced examination of both merits and risks\",\n      \"Concludes with definitive stance\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-05\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Rising Global Obesity and Sedentary Lifestyles\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many countries around the world, obesity rates among both children and adults have increased significantly in recent decades.\\n\\nWhy is this occurring, and what measures can be taken by families and schools to combat this trend?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase prompt & answer both questions\",\n        \"tips\": \"Obesity driven by hyper-palatable processed foods and screen-centric sedentary habits; tackled by nutrition education and compulsory school athletics.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why is this occurring? (Causes)\",\n        \"focus\": \"Dietary shift and screen culture\",\n        \"tips\": \"Ubiquity of ultra-processed fast food high in refined sugars; shift from outdoor physical play to smartphone/gaming screens.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Measures by families and schools\",\n        \"focus\": \"Coordinated parental & school policies\",\n        \"tips\": \"Parents modeling home-cooked meals and limiting screen time; schools banning sugary canteen items and mandating 60 mins of daily sports.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of answer\",\n        \"tips\": \"Reversing obesity demands joint domestic and institutional interventions early in childhood.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The alarming escalation of obesity across global demographics has emerged as one of the defining public health crises of the modern era. This trend is driven principally by the ubiquitous availability of cheap ultra-processed foodstuffs and the rise of screen-dominated sedentary leisure, but it can be substantially reversed through coordinated interventions implemented by parents and educational institutions.\\n\\nThe underlying drivers of modern obesity are both nutritional and behavioral. Over the past three decades, the global food supply has been flooded with hyper-palatable, calorie-dense foods packed with refined sugars, trans-fats, and chemical additives designed to trigger cravings. Coupled with aggressive marketing, these processed options are frequently cheaper and more accessible than fresh produce for time-pressed households. Simultaneously, human energy expenditure has plummeted. The proliferation of digital screens, streaming services, and video games has replaced spontaneous outdoor recreation with hours of immobile physical passivity among youth, creating a chronic caloric imbalance.\\n\\nTo counteract this perilous trajectory, families and schools must establish rigorous healthy environments. Domestically, parents must serve as primary nutritional role models by preparing wholesome home-cooked meals and strictly rationing recreational screen time. Involving children in grocery shopping and meal preparation cultivates early culinary literacy and healthy appetites. Concurrently, educational institutions must enforce structural public health standards. Canteens should ban sugary sodas and deep-fried snacks, replacing them with subsidized fruits, vegetables, and whole grains. Furthermore, schools must incorporate mandatory daily physical education programs—minimum sixty minutes of cardiovascular sports—ensuring that exercise is an integral component of the academic curriculum rather than an optional afterthought.\\n\\nIn conclusion, modern obesity is rooted in the twin hazards of ultra-processed industrial diets and sedentary digital leisure. By transforming school canteen offerings and reinstating active domestic lifestyle habits, families and schools can safeguard the physical wellbeing of future generations.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Answers both prompt questions directly with equal depth. Rich topic-specific vocabulary ('hyper-palatable', 'chronic caloric imbalance', 'culinary literacy'). Flawless cohesion.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Directly answered Question 1 (causes)\",\n      \"Directly answered Question 2 (family & school measures)\",\n      \"Maintained academic register throughout\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-06\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Space Exploration Spending vs Earthly Problems\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Governments across the world spend billions of dollars annually on space exploration programs. Some people argue that this money should instead be devoted to solving urgent problems on Earth, such as poverty and environmental degradation.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge urgent terrestrial needs, but disagree with defunding space exploration, which yields vital climate monitoring and technological spinoffs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why space investment matters for Earth\",\n        \"focus\": \"Earth observation & climate tracking\",\n        \"tips\": \"Satellite arrays monitor deforestation, rising sea levels, ocean heat, and agricultural weather disasters.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Spinoff technologies and resource scarcity\",\n        \"focus\": \"Technological innovation & clean energy\",\n        \"tips\": \"Solar cells, water purification, advanced materials originated from aerospace R&D; space budget is a tiny fraction of military spending.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate opinion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Space science is not an extravagant escape, but an essential diagnostic tool for protecting Earth.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The allocation of colossal national budgets to space exploration frequently draws sharp criticism from those who believe such expenditure is unjustifiable while millions suffer from poverty, hunger, and environmental collapse on Earth. While I acknowledge the profound urgency of terrestrial suffering, I strongly disagree with the notion that funding for space research should be curtailed, as space science provides indispensable diagnostic tools and technological breakthroughs vital for solving Earth's problems.\\n\\nFirst and foremost, satellite infrastructure developed through space programs is the cornerstone of contemporary planetary conservation. Orbital Earth-observation satellites provide the continuous, objective data required to monitor melting polar ice sheets, track deforestation rates in the Amazon, forecast catastrophic typhoons, and manage agricultural irrigation during severe droughts. Without space-based atmospheric sensors, climate scientists would be virtually blind to the real-time dynamics of global warming. Disinvesting in space technology would directly cripple our capacity to formulate effective ecological remediation strategies on the ground.\\n\\nFurthermore, the technological dividends yielded by aerospace research routinely transform mundane terrestrial life. Breakthroughs originally engineered for space missions—including high-efficiency solar photovoltaic cells, advanced water purification filters, lightweight fire-resistant fabrics, and medical imaging technologies—have revolutionized everyday public health and clean energy sectors. Moreover, viewed in financial perspective, space exploration expenditures constitute an infinitesimal fraction of global public spending compared to national defense budgets. Reallocating a fraction of military spending would eradicate global poverty far more effectively than dismantling our scientific portals to the cosmos.\\n\\nIn conclusion, space exploration is not an extravagant luxury disconnected from human welfare; it is an essential investment that equips humanity with the technologies and environmental data needed to heal our planet. Starving space exploration would compromise, rather than accelerate, our ability to solve earthly crises.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Persuasive and mature argument. Clear rebuttal of the counter-argument regarding budget size compared to defense. Band 8+ academic style throughout.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear stance maintained throughout\",\n      \"Addressed the counter-perspective directly\",\n      \"Specific spinoff examples provided (solar cells, water purification)\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-07\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"University Education vs Vocational Practical Apprenticeships\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that obtaining a university degree is the best pathway to career success and financial stability. Others argue that vocational training and practical apprenticeships offer better employment prospects in today's economy.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Discuss merits of academic degree vs skilled trades; argue that vocational pathways now provide equal or superior economic stability without student debt.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case for university education\",\n        \"focus\": \"Professional licensing & intellectual breadth\",\n        \"tips\": \"Medicine, law, academia, engineering legally require accredited degrees; develops critical thinking.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Case for vocational apprenticeships\",\n        \"focus\": \"High demand, zero debt, practical mastery\",\n        \"tips\": \"Chronic shortages of electricians, plumbers, network technicians; apprentices earn while learning, avoiding debilitating student loans.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Nuanced synthesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Both paths have value, but modern economies must discard academic elitism and elevate vocational careers.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The debate regarding the optimal pathway to lifelong career fulfillment has intensified as higher education tuition fees surge alongside shifting labor market demands. While conventional wisdom continues to champion university degrees as the premier route to professional status, I believe that vocational apprenticeships offer comparable, and in many sectors superior, economic stability in the modern economy.\\n\\nOn the one hand, traditional university education remains indispensable for specific elite and regulated professions. Fields such as medicine, civil engineering, jurisprudence, and scientific research require exhaustive theoretical foundations and formal accreditations that can only be conferred by accredited universities. Beyond technical certification, the university experience exposes students to interdisciplinary discourse, cultivating critical analysis, long-form writing, and independent research competencies that prepare graduates for adaptable leadership roles in knowledge-based corporate environments.\\n\\nOn the other hand, the contemporary job market is plagued by widespread graduate underemployment coupled with severe shortages of skilled tradespeople. Many university graduates emerge burdened by debilitating student debt only to discover that degrees in generic humanities or business disciplines do not guarantee employment. In sharp contrast, vocational training and trade apprenticeships—such as precision machining, electrical engineering, plumbing, and network cabling—provide paid, hands-on experiential learning from day one. Because modern societies face an acute scarcity of certified technical specialists, apprentices frequently command substantial starting salaries without owing a penny in tuition debt.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while university degrees remain vital for accredited professional and academic careers, the contemporary labor landscape has elevated vocational apprenticeships into equally lucrative, debt-free, and recession-resilient alternatives. Society must dismantle the antiquated stigma surrounding trade qualifications.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb structural organization. Balanced evaluation of both options followed by a well-reasoned personal opinion. Excellent lexical range ('jurisprudence', 'underemployment', 'recession-resilient').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Discussed university merits (medical/legal accreditations)\",\n      \"Discussed trade merits (debt-free, high demand)\",\n      \"Clear personal judgment in conclusion\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-08\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Compulsory Retirement Age vs Working Indefinitely\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many countries, governments enforce a mandatory retirement age for employees, typically between 60 and 68 years old.\\n\\nDo you agree or disagree that older workers should be forced to retire once they reach a specific age?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Mandatory retirement is discriminatory and economically counterproductive; strongly disagree with compulsory age cutoffs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why mandatory retirement is flawed\",\n        \"focus\": \"Loss of institutional wisdom, ageism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Chronological age does not dictate mental acumen; forcing capable seniors out harms mental wellbeing and wastes valuable institutional experience.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Economic burdens of aging populations\",\n        \"focus\": \"Pension deficits & talent shortages\",\n        \"tips\": \"Shrinking workforces strain pension systems; allowing willing seniors to work part-time or consult relieves state welfare burdens.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate position\",\n        \"tips\": \"Employment should be evaluated on individual competence, health, and choice, not an arbitrary chronological number.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The statutory imposition of mandatory retirement ages has become an increasingly contentious public policy as global life expectancy rises. Although advocates of compulsory retirement argue that it creates career advancement opportunities for younger generations, I completely disagree with forcing capable older workers out of employment solely based on their chronological age.\\n\\nForemost among the reasons to reject compulsory retirement is that chronological age is an arbitrary and unreliable proxy for cognitive competence or professional capability. Modern healthcare advancements mean that many individuals in their late sixties and seventies possess excellent mental acuity, physical vitality, and unmatched institutional wisdom accrued over decades of service. Discarding these seasoned professionals based purely on a calendar date constitutes institutional ageism. Furthermore, abrupt forced retirement frequently precipitates severe psychological decline, loneliness, and loss of purpose among seniors whose identity and social ties are intimately linked to their professional lives.\\n\\nFrom a macroeconomic perspective, compulsory retirement is economically unsustainable in an era of aging populations and plummeting birth rates. Western and Asian economies face acute labor shortages and mounting state pension deficits. Forcing healthy, willing seniors out of the workforce deprives treasuries of income tax revenue while simultaneously multiplying public pension burdens. Conversely, allowing older individuals to continue working—perhaps in flexible, mentoring, or part-time advisory capacities—alleviates fiscal pressure on social welfare networks while enabling vital intergenerational skill transfers to junior colleagues.\\n\\nIn conclusion, forcing older workers into mandatory retirement is both discriminatory and economically reckless. Employment decisions should be determined by individual merit, health, and personal choice, rather than an archaic chronological threshold.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Strong, well-defended thesis. Rich vocabulary ('chronological age', 'unmatched institutional wisdom', 'intergenerational skill transfers'). Flawless logical cohesion.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear stance stated upfront\",\n      \"Refuted counter-argument about youth jobs\",\n      \"Solid macroeconomic evidence provided\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-09\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Preserving Cultural Heritage vs Modern Urban Development\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In rapidly growing cities, historic buildings are frequently demolished to make way for modern high-rise apartments and office towers. Some people argue that preserving historic architecture is essential, while others believe that modern housing and commercial space must take priority.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Both modern urban density and historical preservation matter; adaptive reuse offers the ideal compromise.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case for modern development\",\n        \"focus\": \"Housing shortages, energy efficiency\",\n        \"tips\": \"Old buildings cannot house dense populations; modern towers have elevators, insulation, and solar capacity.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Case for preserving heritage\",\n        \"focus\": \"Cultural identity, aesthetics, tourism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Historic structures anchor civic identity, attract tourism, and provide irreplaceable aesthetic character.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis via adaptive reuse\",\n        \"tips\": \"Cities should not choose between past and future; retrofitting historic facades into modern interiors balances both needs.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The relentless pace of urbanization in modern metropolises has sparked intense friction between the preservation of historical architecture and the urgent demand for contemporary commercial and residential infrastructure. While proponents of modernization emphasize the necessity of high-density housing, I believe that historic edifices form the soul of a city and that intelligent adaptive reuse can reconcile these competing imperatives.\\n\\nOn the one hand, advocates of demolition present compelling demographic arguments. Global cities face unprecedented population influxes that result in severe housing deficits and exorbitant rental markets. Traditional low-rise historical structures occupy valuable downtown real estate while accommodating only a handful of occupants. Replacing them with modern high-rise towers maximizes urban land efficiency, provides thousands of energy-efficient apartments, and reduces commuting times by clustering citizens near public transit nodes. Furthermore, ancient buildings frequently fail modern earthquake and fire safety regulations, requiring exorbitant maintenance expenditures that cash-strapped municipalities struggle to fund.\\n\\nOn the other hand, razing historic landmarks inflicts irreparable cultural harm. Architecture serves as physical history—a tangible embodiment of a community's aesthetic heritage, craftsmanship, and civic identity. A city comprised solely of identical glass-and-steel skyscrapers forfeits its unique character, degenerating into an indistinguishable, soulless concrete grid. Furthermore, historical districts are potent economic engines for cultural tourism, drawing millions of international visitors whose spending sustains local hospitality enterprises. Preserving structures like Victorian terraces or ancient temple precincts fosters civic pride and connects future generations to their historical roots.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although the demand for modern housing density is undeniable, completely obliterating historic architecture strips a city of its cultural soul. Municipal planners should mandate adaptive reuse, retrofitting historic exteriors with modern, high-density interiors to honor the past while embracing future growth.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Nuanced discussion introducing adaptive reuse as an innovative synthesis. Rich topic vocabulary ('edifices', 'adaptive reuse', 'tangible embodiment', 'soulless concrete grid').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Discussed both modern density and cultural preservation\",\n      \"Clear synthesis (adaptive reuse)\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-10\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Youth Unemployment in the Age of Automation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many parts of the world, rates of youth unemployment are substantially higher than the national average, leaving millions of young graduates unable to secure stable employment.\\n\\nWhat are the primary causes of youth unemployment, and what measures should be taken to solve this problem?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase problem\",\n        \"tips\": \"Youth unemployment caused by academic-industry mismatch and economic automation; solved by curriculum reform and subsidized internships.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Primary causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Curriculum disconnect & experience paradox\",\n        \"tips\": \"Outdated university syllabuses disconnected from digital economy; entry-level jobs requiring 3+ years experience.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Practical solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Vocational integration & hiring incentives\",\n        \"tips\": \"Governments partner with tech firms to offer paid apprenticeships; tax credits for enterprises employing youth.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of actions\",\n        \"tips\": \"Bridging the gap between classroom theory and workplace skills is essential to unlocking young talent.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The disproportionately high incidence of unemployment among young adults represents one of the most perilous socioeconomic challenges confronting contemporary societies. This crisis is rooted in an acute disconnect between academic curricula and labor market demands, exacerbated by entry-level experience barriers. However, it can be mitigated through institutional vocational integration and government-subsidized apprenticeship programs.\\n\\nThe genesis of youth unemployment lies predominantly in educational obsolescence. Many tertiary institutions continue to teach theoretical, antiquated syllabuses that fail to equip graduates with the digital, technical, and interpersonal skills sought by modern employers. Consequently, millions of students graduate with degrees in oversaturated fields, possessing zero practical fluency in emerging industries like renewable engineering or data analytics. Furthermore, young jobseekers confront the notorious 'experience paradox': employers demand several years of verified workplace experience even for nominal entry-level vacancies, effectively locking out recent graduates who lack professional networks or family connections.\\n\\nTo remediate this structural failure, educational systems and state policymakers must implement concerted reforms. First, universities must aggressively overhaul curricula in active collaboration with industry leaders, integrating compulsory paid internships, coding literacy, and project-based problem solving into all degree programs. This guarantees that students graduate with tangible workplace competencies. Second, governments should introduce targeted tax incentives and wage subsidies for enterprises that recruit and train workers under twenty-five, making it financially attractive for companies to mentor inexperienced talent rather than outsourcing positions.\\n\\nIn conclusion, youth unemployment is catalyzed by educational irrelevance and unrealistic entry requirements. By synchronizing academic training with industry realities and subsidizing youth hiring, governments can ensure that the next generation successfully transitions into productive careers.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Clearly identifies specific causes (experience paradox, outdated syllabuses) and provides viable, actionable solutions (curriculum overhaul, hiring tax credits).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear cause-and-solution structure\",\n      \"Addressed both education and employer behavior\",\n      \"Flawless academic tone\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-11\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Government Subsidies for the Arts vs Scientific Research\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people argue that during times of economic austerity, governments should cease subsidizing the creative arts (theatre, music, and painting) and redirect all funding to scientific research and healthcare.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge the undeniable importance of healthcare and science, but strongly disagree with eliminating arts funding.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why science is essential\",\n        \"focus\": \"Medical progress and economic return\",\n        \"tips\": \"Science develops vaccines, green energy, and infrastructure; deserves substantial investment.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Why arts remain vital even in crisis\",\n        \"focus\": \"Mental health, civic unity, creative economy\",\n        \"tips\": \"The arts provide solace, combat social alienation, stimulate tourism, and cultivate creative thinking.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate balanced stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Science extends human life, but the arts make life worth living; both require sustained public support.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"During periods of fiscal contraction, public expenditure on cultural and artistic institutions frequently comes under intense scrutiny, with critics contending that state grants for theatre, fine arts, and music should be terminated in favor of vital scientific and medical research. While I agree that healthcare and scientific advancement are paramount societal imperatives, I strongly disagree with the complete elimination of public funding for the creative arts.\\n\\nIt is indisputable that scientific research must receive generous budgetary allocations. Investment in biotechnology, oncology, renewable energy, and medical infrastructure directly saves lives, extends human longevity, and drives macroeconomic competitiveness. When public health emergencies occur, it is medical researchers—not artists—who manufacture life-saving vaccines and diagnostic protocols. Therefore, the prioritization of scientific innovation is an essential public duty that no responsible administration can neglect.\\n\\nHowever, stripping all funding from the creative arts represents a dangerously shortsighted policy. The arts are not a decorative luxury; they are the bedrock of cultural identity, social cohesion, and psychological resilience. During historical crises, music, literature, and visual arts have provided solace, elevated public morale, and united divided populations. Moreover, the cultural sector is a powerful economic generator: public subsidies for municipal galleries, provincial theatres, and musical ensembles stimulate billions in ancillary revenue for domestic hospitality and tourism industries. Finally, exposure to artistic expression nurtures divergent thinking and creativity, cognitive attributes that directly stimulate inventive scientific breakthroughs.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while science and healthcare undeniably warrant substantial public investment, eliminating artistic funding would impoverish society's cultural soul and weaken the creative economy. A healthy nation requires science to preserve human life, and the arts to make life worth living.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Beautiful balance between acknowledging the importance of science while vigorously defending the arts. Strong memorable concluding sentence.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear stance stated upfront\",\n      \"Balanced evaluation of scientific priorities\",\n      \"Compelling defense of cultural subsidies\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-12\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"International Tourism: Cultural Exchange vs Environmental Degradation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"International tourism has expanded tremendously in recent decades. Some people believe this brings valuable economic growth and mutual cultural understanding, while others argue that it causes irreparable environmental damage and cultural erosion.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Mass tourism generates vital income and dismantles prejudices, but unchecked overtourism damages delicate ecosystems; eco-tourism regulations are vital.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Economic & cultural benefits\",\n        \"focus\": \"Job creation & cross-cultural empathy\",\n        \"tips\": \"Provides foreign exchange revenue to developing communities; breaks down xenophobia through human contact.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Environmental & social harms\",\n        \"focus\": \"Carbon footprint, pollution, commercialization\",\n        \"tips\": \"Aviation emissions, waste overload on fragile islands, cultural commercialization, rising local living costs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis via sustainable tourism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Tourism should not be banned, but strictly regulated with visitor caps and tourist eco-taxes.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The exponential boom in global travel over the past half-century has transformed international tourism into one of the largest worldwide economic sectors. While proponents celebrate tourism for fostering cross-cultural goodwill and generating regional prosperity, critics highlight its disastrous ecological footprint and cultural commodification. In my view, while tourism's cultural and economic benefits are immense, they must be strictly governed by sustainable tourist caps to avert permanent environmental devastation.\\n\\nOn the one hand, international travel acts as an unparalleled catalyst for economic vitality and cosmopolitan empathy. For many developing island nations and historic regions, tourism accounts for a substantial proportion of gross domestic product, funding public hospitals, schools, and transportation networks while employing millions of hospitality workers. On a cultural level, direct physical contact between diverse populations dismantles provincial stereotypes and nurtures international understanding. When visitors experience unfamiliar traditions, cuisines, and historical monuments firsthand, it fosters mutual tolerance and appreciation for global diversity.\\n\\nOn the other hand, unchecked mass tourism inflicts catastrophic ecological and social damage. The carbon emissions generated by long-haul aviation and mega-cruise liners accelerate global climate destabilization. Furthermore, fragile natural ecosystems—such as Thailand's coral reefs or alpine hiking trails—suffer irreversible degradation under the trampling of millions of holidaymakers. Socially, overtourism often sparks severe housing crises: residential properties are converted into unregulated short-term holiday rentals, inflating rents and pricing indigenous residents out of historic city centers like Venice and Barcelona. Moreover, sacred indigenous customs are frequently reduced to trivialized commercial performances for selfie-taking travelers.\\n\\nIn conclusion, international tourism is a double-edged sword. While its economic contributions and capacity to dissolve cultural barriers are extraordinary, its unmanaged growth threatens the very destinations travelers admire. Governments must implement stringent environmental levies and daily visitor quotas to preserve destinations for future generations.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Superb exploration of both sides. Sophisticated vocabulary ('cosmopolitan empathy', 'commodification', 'double-edged sword'). Clear personal recommendation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explored both economic benefits and ecological damage\",\n      \"Specific real-world examples (Venice, Barcelona, aviation)\",\n      \"Clear regulatory conclusion\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-13\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Advertising Directed at Children\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Every day, children are exposed to massive amounts of commercial advertising, especially for fast food, sugary drinks, and expensive toys.\\n\\nSome people believe that advertising aimed at young children should be completely banned by law.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Children lack cognitive defenses against deceptive advertising; strongly agree that commercial ads aimed at children should be prohibited.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Psychological vulnerability\",\n        \"focus\": \"Inability to discern commercial intent\",\n        \"tips\": \"Developmental psychology shows children under 8 cannot differentiate entertainment from sales pitches; creates relentless pester power.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Public health consequences\",\n        \"focus\": \"Childhood obesity and materialism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Heavy promotion of ultra-processed junk food and expensive toys fosters early health crises and feelings of inadequacy.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Legislation, as successfully enacted in Sweden and Quebec, is essential to protect vulnerable minors.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"In the modern digital landscape, children are inundated daily by sophisticated commercial advertising campaigns promoting fast food, carbonated beverages, and plastic toys. Because children lack the psychological defenses to evaluate marketing claims critically, I completely agree with the proposition that all commercial advertising targeted at young children should be prohibited by law.\\n\\nThe foremost argument for a total ban is rooted in child developmental psychology. Extensive pediatric research demonstrates that children under the age of eight cannot discern commercial intent; they perceive advertisements not as persuasive sales pitches, but as objective, trustworthy information. Advertisers exploit this developmental vulnerability by deploying colorful animated mascots, catchy jingles, and celebrity endorsements, deliberately cultivating relentless 'pester power'—wherein children emotionally manipulate parents into purchasing unneeded products. Preying upon the cognitive immaturity of minors to drive corporate profit is ethically reprehensible and warrants strict statutory prohibition.\\n\\nFurthermore, advertising directed at children has generated severe public health and social repercussions. The overwhelming preponderance of child-targeted commercials promotes foods high in refined sugar, saturated fats, and sodium, directly fueling the epidemic of childhood obesity and juvenile Type 2 diabetes. Simultaneously, the relentless marketing of expensive video games and branded merchandise inculcates toxic consumerism and materialism from infancy, causing low-income children to suffer acute feelings of social inadequacy when their families cannot afford advertised status symbols. Jurisdictions such as Sweden and the Canadian province of Quebec have already enacted comprehensive bans on commercial advertising to children under thirteen, demonstrating that such legislation successfully reduces child-focused commercial exploitation without harming general economic vitality.\\n\\nIn conclusion, because children lack the cognitive discernment to defend themselves against manipulative marketing, and because child advertising fuels serious physical and mental health crises, governments should enact comprehensive legal bans against commercial advertising aimed at minors.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Flawless argument from developmental psychology and public health. Concrete statutory precedents cited (Sweden, Quebec). Band 8+ accuracy.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear agree position maintained\",\n      \"Addressed psychological vulnerability and health impacts\",\n      \"Cited real-world legislative precedent\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-14\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Criminal Justice: Harsh Punishment vs Educational Rehabilitation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that long prison sentences and harsh punishments are the most effective way to reduce crime in society. Others argue that education, job training, and rehabilitation programs are much more effective.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Both punitive deterrence and rehabilitation have roles; argue that rehabilitative models (like Scandinavia) drastically outperform harsh incarceration in reducing recidivism.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case for punitive deterrence\",\n        \"focus\": \"Retribution, public safety, deterrence\",\n        \"tips\": \"Keeps violent offenders off streets; provides justice for victims; deters potential criminals.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Case for rehabilitation\",\n        \"focus\": \"Education, job skills, mental health\",\n        \"tips\": \"Harsh prisons create 'colleges of crime' with high recidivism; vocational training and therapy allow offenders to reintegrate productively.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Harsh detention is needed for dangerous violent offenders, but educational rehabilitation must be the primary tool to permanently reduce crime.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The persistent challenge of criminality has polarized public debate over whether penal systems should prioritize punitive incarceration or therapeutic rehabilitation. While proponents of punitive justice maintain that lengthy prison sentences deter prospective offenders, I believe that rehabilitation through vocational education and psychological counseling is overwhelmingly more effective at permanently reducing crime.\\n\\nOn the one hand, advocates of severe custodial sentencing emphasize retributive justice and immediate public safety. Isolating dangerous and violent offenders behind bars physically prevents them from reoffending during the duration of their sentence, offering vital protection and closure to victims of crime. Furthermore, proponents argue that harsh prison conditions serve as a formidable psychological deterrent, signaling to potential lawbreakers that criminal conduct incurs intolerable personal costs. For severe offenses such as murder or aggravated assault, society rightly demands a proportionate punitive response that upholds the moral authority of the law.\\n\\nOn the other hand, empirical evidence demonstrates that harsh, purely punitive incarceration exacerbates rather than cures criminality. Overcrowded, dehumanizing prisons frequently act as 'universities of crime', where minor offenders are brutalized and indoctrinated into hardened criminal networks. Upon release with a criminal record and zero marketable skills, former inmates face widespread societal ostracization, rendering reoffending almost inevitable. In contrast, rehabilitative penal systems—such as those pioneered in Norway and Sweden—focus on vocational training, literacy education, and addiction therapy within humane custodial environments. By equipping inmates with accredited trades and psychological coping mechanisms, these progressive systems achieve recidivism rates below twenty percent, compared to over sixty percent in strictly punitive jurisdictions.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while custodial detention remains necessary to incapacitate dangerous violent criminals, relying solely on harsh punishment fails to address the root socioeconomic drivers of crime. Prioritizing education, vocational skills, and psychological rehabilitation transforms former offenders into contributing citizens, offering the only sustainable pathway to a safer society.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Thoughtful, balanced, mature essay. Clear statistical comparison (Scandinavia <20% recidivism vs punitive jurisdictions >60%). High-level vocabulary.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explored both deterrence and rehabilitation\",\n      \"Clear personal stance throughout\",\n      \"Used comparative statistical examples\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-15\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Social Media and Adolescent Mental Health\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Over the past decade, adolescents around the world have spent increasing amounts of their daily lives on social media platforms. Many experts argue that this trend has contributed to a surge in anxiety, depression, and low self-esteem among teenagers.\\n\\nWhat are the main reasons why social media impacts adolescent mental health so negatively, and what practical solutions can be adopted to address this issue?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase prompt & answer both parts\",\n        \"tips\": \"Social media damages teen wellbeing via algorithmic comparison traps and sleep disruption; mitigated through digital curfews and school phone bans.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why does it harm teen mental health? (Reasons)\",\n        \"focus\": \"Toxic social comparison & sleep deprivation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Curated highlight reels spark feelings of inadequacy; nighttime blue light and notification dopamine loops ruin sleep.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: What practical solutions can be adopted? (Solutions)\",\n        \"focus\": \"Platform age-gating & school phone-free zones\",\n        \"tips\": \"Strict age verification by tech platforms; schools implementing bell-to-bell phone bans; parents establishing tech-free bedrooms.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of answer\",\n        \"tips\": \"Protecting adolescents requires technological guardrails combined with domestic boundaries.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The ubiquity of social media platforms has transformed the social fabric of teenage life, coinciding with a deeply troubling spike in adolescent anxiety, depression, and clinical loneliness. This negative psychological fallout is caused primarily by toxic social comparison and chronic sleep deprivation, but it can be substantially mitigated through institutional digital regulations and domestic tech boundaries.\\n\\nThe mechanisms through which social media degrades adolescent mental health are insidious. First, image-centric platforms subject vulnerable teenagers to a ceaseless barrage of digitally enhanced, curated 'highlight reels' of peers and influencers. Adolescents—whose brains are developmentally hypersensitive to peer acceptance—inevitably internalize unrealistic standards of physical beauty, wealth, and popularity, breeding intense feelings of inadequacy, body dysmorphia, and fear of missing out (FOMO). Second, algorithmic notification loops engineered to maximize screen time invade the nocturnal hours. Late-night smartphone usage disrupts circadian rhythms through blue-light melatonin suppression, causing chronic sleep deficit, which is a verified clinical precursor to adolescent depressive disorders.\\n\\nTo counteract this crisis, decisive structural and domestic solutions must be mobilized. At the statutory level, governments must compel social media corporations to enforce robust age-verification mechanisms and eliminate addictive algorithmic design features—such as infinite scroll and autoplay—for users under eighteen. Furthermore, educational authorities should implement mandatory 'bell-to-bell' smartphone bans across middle and high schools, requiring devices to be locked away during the school day to foster real-world social interaction and focused learning. Domestically, parents must institute firm digital hygiene protocols, notably banning smartphones from bedrooms after 9 PM to safeguard vital sleep cycles.\\n\\nIn conclusion, social media damages adolescent psychological health by fueling toxic comparison and eviscerating restorative sleep. By enacting statutory platform protections and restoring screen-free school and bedroom sanctuaries, society can insulate teenagers from digital distress.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Comprehensive answer addressing both reasons and solutions. Excellent psychological and clinical vocabulary ('body dysmorphia', 'circadian rhythms', 'melatonin suppression', 'bell-to-bell smartphone bans').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Directly addressed reasons in Body 1\",\n      \"Directly addressed solutions in Body 2\",\n      \"Cohesive and persuasive throughout\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-16\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Remote Work and Traditional Office Culture\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many industries, employees are increasingly working remotely from home rather than commuting to physical commercial offices.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree that the permanent shift toward remote work is beneficial for both employees and employers?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase topic & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"State strong agreement that remote work yields substantial mutual advantages in productivity and cost reduction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Employee benefits\",\n        \"focus\": \"Autonomy, commute reduction, well-being\",\n        \"tips\": \"Eliminating lengthy commutes reduces stress, saves money, and enhances work-life balance.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Employer benefits\",\n        \"focus\": \"Overhead savings, talent acquisition\",\n        \"tips\": \"Lower commercial leasing costs and ability to recruit talent globally regardless of location.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate thesis & future outlook\",\n        \"tips\": \"Conclude that with robust digital infrastructure, telecommuting represents an enduring win-win.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The acceleration of telecommunications technology has precipitated a paradigm shift in contemporary labor dynamics, prompting millions of professionals to abandon daily office commutes in favor of telecommuting. I firmly believe that this permanent transition to remote work is highly advantageous for workers and corporate entities alike, as it optimizes personal well-being while drastically diminishing corporate overhead.\\n\\nFrom the perspective of employees, teleworking eradicates the physical and psychological toll associated with urban commuting. In major metropolitan centers, workers routinely expend several hours daily navigating congested transport networks, a routine that induces chronic fatigue and erodes leisure time. By operating from home, professionals reclaim substantial temporal autonomy, enabling them to prepare nutritious meals, pursue physical exercise, and fulfill familial responsibilities. Furthermore, autonomy over one's immediate physical environment often fosters deeper cognitive concentration, shielding knowledge workers from the pervasive distractions, impromptu interruptions, and ambient noise characteristic of open-plan commercial offices.\\n\\nEqually compelling are the operational dividends accrued by employers. Conventional office premises in metropolitan business districts represent exorbitant capital expenditures encompassing commercial rent, utility bills, and facility maintenance. By embracing distributed workforces, companies can downsize their real estate footprints and reallocate capital into technological innovation and competitive compensation packages. Moreover, geographical decentralization dismantles hiring boundaries, allowing enterprises to recruit exceptional talent across diverse jurisdictions rather than being restricted to candidates residing within commuting radius.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the permanent migration toward remote work heralds an overwhelmingly progressive evolution in occupational culture. By simultaneously improving employee morale and granting organizations leaner cost structures and broader talent pools, distributed employment models represent a mutually beneficial arrangement for the modern economy.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. The essay exhibits sophisticated lexicon ('precipitated a paradigm shift', 'temporal autonomy', 'exorbitant capital expenditures'). Cohesive devices are varied and fluid, and the thesis is defended with balanced rigor.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clear opinion established early\",\n      \"Separated employee and employer arguments logically\",\n      \"Rich academic collocations\",\n      \"Concluded without introducing new points\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-17\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Space Exploration vs Poverty Alleviation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people argue that spending billions of dollars on space exploration is unjustified when there are pressing issues such as poverty and disease on Earth. Others believe that space research is vital for the future of humanity.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce controversy & state position\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge urgency of humanitarian relief, but argue space exploration creates indispensable technological spin-offs.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case against space expenditure\",\n        \"focus\": \"Immediate human suffering, fiscal urgency\",\n        \"tips\": \"Poverty, malnutrition, water scarcity require immediate direct capital allocation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Case for space exploration\",\n        \"focus\": \"Technological dividends & long-term survival\",\n        \"tips\": \"Satellite weather tracking, medical advances, clean energy innovation stem from aerospace R&D.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis & balanced verdict\",\n        \"tips\": \"Space funding is not a wasteful extravagance but a dual-purpose investment in terrestrial resilience.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The allocation of colossal budgetary resources toward extraterrestrial exploration frequently sparks contentious ethical debate. While critics maintain that cosmic endeavors constitute an unconscionable extravagance in the face of rampant poverty and medical deprivation on Earth, I maintain that space science delivers indispensable technological dividends and foundational knowledge essential for safeguarding humanity's long-term survival.\\n\\nOn the one hand, opponents of aerospace investment put forth an intuitively persuasive moral argument centered on human misery. In numerous developing territories, millions of people languish without reliable access to potable drinking water, primary healthcare, or adequate nutritional sustenance. From this vantage point, channeling tens of billions of public dollars into planetary rovers and deep-space telescopes appears profoundly misaligned with urgent humanitarian priorities. Diverting even a portion of aerospace budgets toward infectious disease eradication, rural electrification, and educational infrastructure could yield immediate, quantifiable improvements in the living standards of society's most vulnerable cohorts.\\n\\nOn the other hand, proponents cogently argue that space exploration is not merely an abstract quest for cosmic discovery, but a proven engine of terrestrial technological breakthrough. The rigorous engineering demands of spaceflight have historically yielded revolutionary spin-off innovations, ranging from advanced water purification systems and solar photovoltaic cells to MRI medical scanners and telecommunications satellites. Earth-observation satellites are directly instrumental in monitoring climate patterns, predicting agricultural drought cycles, and coordinating disaster relief during catastrophic floods. Far from draining resources away from Earth, aerospace research furnishes the very instruments needed to manage planetary environmental challenges.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while mitigating acute poverty on our home planet is an indisputable imperative, abandoning space research would represent short-sighted negligence. Because space science generates critical technologies that directly empower terrestrial sustainability, international governments should pursue a balanced approach that funds both immediate social welfare and visionary scientific discovery.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Demonstrates excellent thematic progression, exceptional paragraph coherence, and high-level lexical sophistication ('unconscionable extravagance', 'potable drinking water', 'terrestrial sustainability').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Addressed both viewpoints fully\",\n      \"Gave nuanced personal stance\",\n      \"Used concrete examples of space spin-offs (water filtration, satellites)\",\n      \"Accurate grammatical range\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-18\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Fast Fashion and Environmental Degradation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The rapid turnover of inexpensive clothing, known as fast fashion, has become popular worldwide. However, it causes severe environmental degradation and generates massive textile waste.\\n\\nWhat are the main causes of this trend, and what measures could be taken to reduce its negative impact?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase phenomenon & signpost\",\n        \"tips\": \"Introduce hyper-consumerism and disposable garments; outline economic and regulatory interventions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Root causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Ultra-low prices, aggressive digital marketing\",\n        \"tips\": \"Algorithmic social media trends and cheap synthetic fabrics like polyester encourage disposable mentalities.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Effective solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Producer responsibility legislation & circular economy\",\n        \"tips\": \"Mandate garment recycling fees, tax non-biodegradable synthetic fibers, promote thrift and clothing rental.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of remedies\",\n        \"tips\": \"Combine corporate statutory accountability with consumer mindfulness.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The global apparel industry has undergone a radical transformation characterized by the ascendancy of fast fashion—the mass production of low-cost, trend-driven garments designed for ephemeral use. This business model has generated catastrophic environmental externalities, including toxic chemical runoff and overflowing municipal landfills. This essay will examine the principal drivers behind this phenomenon before delineating pragmatic corrective interventions.\\n\\nThe proliferation of fast fashion stems primarily from predatory marketing algorithms and the economic ubiquity of synthetic petroleum-based textiles. Modern fashion retail conglomerates leverage social media influencers and micro-trend algorithms to instill an artificial sense of obsolescence, convincing youthful consumers that garments worn more than a few times are out of date. Simultaneously, the extensive deployment of virgin polyester and cheap petrochemical dyes allows manufacturers to compress production expenses to negligible levels. Because garments are priced so cheaply, consumers perceive clothing as disposable commodities rather than durable assets, discarding worn items after negligible usage.\\n\\nTo counter this unsustainable trajectory, comprehensive legislative mandates and circular economy initiatives must be enacted. First, governments ought to institute Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) regulations that hold apparel manufacturers financially accountable for the end-of-life disposal of their merchandise. Imposing a statutory tariff on virgin synthetic textiles would incentivize corporations to invest in durable, biodegradable natural fibers and closed-loop garment recycling facilities. On a civic level, public awareness campaigns should actively champion the resurgence of garment repair, vintage thrift shopping, and subscription-based clothing rental services, thereby decelerating overall consumer acquisition rates.\\n\\nTo conclude, the fast fashion crisis is fueled by algorithmically engineered consumerism and inexpensive synthetic manufacturing. Curtailing this ecological menace necessitates robust regulatory frameworks that penalize corporate waste, alongside a cultural shift toward conscious, durable stewardship of textiles.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Exemplary cause-and-solution structure. Features precise domain terminology ('catastrophic environmental externalities', 'petroleum-based textiles', 'Extended Producer Responsibility').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Clearly identified multiple distinct causes\",\n      \"Provided practical, enforceable solutions\",\n      \"Met word count requirement\",\n      \"Maintained formal academic tone throughout\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-19\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Transition to a Cashless Society\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Many countries are moving toward a cashless society where all financial transactions are conducted electronically using cards, smartphones, or online platforms.\\n\\nDo the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase topic & declare thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"State clearly that while privacy and digital exclusion are real risks, the security and efficiency advantages prevail.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Disadvantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Financial surveillance, digital divide, cybersecurity\",\n        \"tips\": \"Elderly citizens struggle with tech; cybercrime vulnerabilities and loss of transactional privacy.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Convenience, curtailing black market, transparency\",\n        \"tips\": \"Instant settlements, automated accounting, elimination of cash robbery, reduced tax evasion.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Weighing conclusion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Advantages outweigh disadvantages provided protective safety nets exist for vulnerable demographics.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The pervasive digitalization of global commerce has accelerated the transition toward a cashless society, wherein physical banknotes and coins are systematically supplanted by contactless cards, mobile wallets, and decentralized electronic ledgers. While this transition entails non-trivial vulnerabilities regarding data privacy and socioeconomic exclusion, I am convinced that the systemic efficiencies, enhanced security, and fiscal transparency of electronic payments comfortably outweigh the drawbacks.\\n\\nAdmittedly, an entirely cashless economy introduces legitimate social and technical concerns. The foremost peril is the disenfranchisement of digitally illiterate or unbanked populations, particularly elderly citizens and marginalized rural dwellers who lack internet connectivity or sophisticated smartphones. If physical tender is abruptly phased out, these demographics risk being completely excluded from essential commercial transactions. Furthermore, centralized digital transactions generate vast digital footprints that financial institutions and surveillance apparatuses can monitor, compromising personal privacy. The consolidation of monetary assets within digital networks also exposes national financial infrastructure to sophisticated cyberattacks, identity theft, and systemic power grid outages.\\n\\nNonetheless, the multifaceted advantages of electronic transactions remain decisive. Chief among these is the dramatic curtailment of illicit financial networks, such as tax evasion, money laundering, and violent street robberies. Physical cash offers anonymity that facilitates black-market transactions, whereas digital payments leave auditable electronic trails that enable tax authorities to track revenue accurately, thereby boosting national public funding for welfare and infrastructure. Furthermore, for businesses and everyday citizens, electronic settlements eliminate the administrative friction, logistical expense, and physical risk of transporting and securing cash deposits, streamlining economic velocity and cross-border trade.\\n\\nIn conclusion, despite genuine hazards concerning digital literacy and surveillance, the transition toward a cashless paradigm provides indispensable institutional benefits. Provided governments maintain protective regulatory guardrails and accessible offline contingencies for vulnerable populations, the advantages of a cashless society overwhelmingly surpass its disadvantages.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Directly answers the prompt's evaluation question ('outweigh'). Demonstrates exceptional mastery of complex grammatical structures and financial terminology.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explicitly answered whether advantages outweigh disadvantages\",\n      \"Addressed both sides with specific details\",\n      \"Maintained logical weighting throughout\",\n      \"Clear academic phrasing\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-20\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Declining Reading Habits Among Young People\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many nations, fewer young people read long-form books for pleasure than in previous generations, preferring instead to spend their leisure time on digital media and short-form videos.\\n\\nWhy is this the case?\\nWhat are the consequences of this trend for individuals and society?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase prompt & outline answers\",\n        \"tips\": \"Identify algorithmic digital entertainment as the cause; identify cognitive fragmentation and reduced empathy as consequences.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why it is happening\",\n        \"focus\": \"Hyper-stimulating content, dopamine loops\",\n        \"tips\": \"Smartphones deliver instant visual gratification, making reading books feel laborious.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Consequences\",\n        \"focus\": \"Shortened attention spans, impoverished vocabulary\",\n        \"tips\": \"Decline in critical thinking, sustained concentration, and societal literacy depth.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Recap both points\",\n        \"tips\": \"Reiterate causes and warn of long-term intellectual atrophy without proactive reading promotion.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The cultural habit of reading long-form literature for recreation has witnessed a pronounced decline among younger demographics, overshadowed by the ubiquitous consumption of short-form digital entertainment. This essay will examine the underlying psychological triggers responsible for this shift before elucidating its grave ramifications for intellectual and civic cohesion.\\n\\nThe primary catalyst behind this behavioral transformation is the hyper-engineered sensory stimulation offered by algorithmic social media and streaming platforms. Applications such as TikTok and YouTube deliver continuous micro-bursts of visual and auditory novelty within seconds, inducing instantaneous dopamine surges in the adolescent brain. In sharp contrast, reading a book demands disciplined cognitive effort, prolonged patience, and imaginative visualization before emotional gratification is achieved. Consequently, adolescents conditioned by rapid algorithmic feeds perceive printed literature as an excruciatingly slow and mentally taxing endeavor, organically drifting toward frictionless digital entertainment.\\n\\nThe ramifications of this trend are deeply troubling for individual cognition and broader society. On an individual level, the abandonment of long-form reading erodes the capacity for sustained attention, critical contemplation, and complex verbal reasoning. Reading literature exercises deep neural pathways associated with empathy, as it requires readers to mentally inhabit the lived perspectives and emotional landscapes of diverse characters. On a societal level, a populace conditioned solely by brief, disjointed visual snippets becomes increasingly susceptible to superficial political rhetoric, polarization, and misinformation, as complex geopolitical and ethical problems cannot be adequately synthesized in sixty-second videos.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the retreat from literary engagement is driven by the addictive architecture of modern digital entertainment. The ensuing degradation of deep cognitive focus and empathetic imagination threatens to produce a society less equipped to grapple with the multifaceted complexities of the modern world.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Directly answers both questions in dedicated body paragraphs. Uses vivid academic vocabulary ('hyper-engineered sensory stimulation', 'cognitive contemplation', 'intellectual and civic cohesion').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Addressed Question 1 (causes) thoroughly in Body 1\",\n      \"Addressed Question 2 (consequences) thoroughly in Body 2\",\n      \"Well-developed paragraph structure\",\n      \"Smooth topic transition\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-21\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"University Tuition Fees vs Free Higher Education\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that university education should be completely free for all students, funded entirely by the state. Others argue that students should pay their own tuition fees because higher education primarily benefits the individual.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree that higher education should be tuition-free?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & clear stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Disagree with purely student-funded model; state strong agreement that free higher education is a vital public investment.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Societal returns on investment\",\n        \"focus\": \"Tax generation, innovation, healthcare\",\n        \"tips\": \"Educated graduates drive high-value industries, generate substantial tax revenue, and elevate public health.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Meritocracy and social mobility\",\n        \"focus\": \"Eradicating debt barriers for underprivileged\",\n        \"tips\": \"Tuition fees entrench socioeconomic inequality by deterring capable working-class students.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate position\",\n        \"tips\": \"Free tuition is not an expense but a high-yielding national investment.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The financial architecture of tertiary education represents a critical axis of public policy debate globally. While opponents of state-funded university models contend that personal career advancement justifies private tuition fees, I wholeheartedly agree that higher education should be fully financed by the state, as an educated populace constitutes an indispensable collective investment that yields immense societal and economic dividends.\\n\\nFirst and foremost, universal access to tertiary education serves as a powerful engine of macroeconomic expansion and national innovation. Modern knowledge economies depend fundamentally on highly skilled workforces comprising engineers, biomedical researchers, educators, and software architects. When university education is tuition-free, as is exemplified in Scandinavian nations like Norway and Germany, access is governed purely by intellectual merit rather than socioeconomic pedigree. Consequently, capable students from impoverished backgrounds can acquire sophisticated specialized skills without bearing crushing debt burdens, driving technological dynamism and generating high-tier income taxes that far surpass the initial educational costs borne by the state.\\n\\nFurthermore, state-funded higher education is essential for dismantling entrenched generational inequality and fostering genuine social mobility. When universities impose exorbitant tuition rates, as witnessed in the United States and the United Kingdom, tertiary credentials become commodified privileges accessible predominantly to affluent families. Working-class graduates, conversely, are shackled with decades of debt repayment, which discourages them from entering vital public-interest professions such as nursing, social work, or legal aid. Eliminating tuition barriers democratizes intellectual advancement and fosters a more equitable, cohesion-oriented social fabric.\\n\\nIn conclusion, framing tertiary education merely as a private commodity overlooks its profound societal externalities. Because state-funded universities cultivate national productivity, stimulate scientific innovation, and guarantee meritocratic social mobility, higher education should be treated as a fundamental public good rather than a commercial transaction.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Lucid and persuasive argument with compelling international examples. Flawless coherence and exceptional lexical range ('socioeconomic pedigree', 'commodified privileges', 'meritocratic social mobility').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Firm agreement stance maintained\",\n      \"Two strong thematic body paragraphs\",\n      \"Real-world international context incorporated\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-22\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Preserving Indigenous Languages vs Global Lingua Franca\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Every year, several minority and indigenous languages become extinct as global languages like English become more dominant. Some people believe that governments should spend public money to preserve endangered languages, while others argue that having fewer languages facilitates global communication and unity.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce linguistic shift & state opinion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge pragmatic benefits of lingua franca, but argue language preservation is vital for human heritage.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Arguments for linguistic convergence\",\n        \"focus\": \"Efficiency, diplomacy, commerce\",\n        \"tips\": \"Common language reduces translation costs, streamlines international trade, and minimizes friction.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Arguments for indigenous language preservation\",\n        \"focus\": \"Ecological knowledge, cultural identity\",\n        \"tips\": \"Languages encode unique philosophies, botanical wisdom, and psychological heritage that cannot be translated.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Balanced verdict\",\n        \"tips\": \"Humanity can embrace a global lingua franca while simultaneously protecting linguistic biodiversity.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The steady erosion of linguistic diversity in favor of global vehicular languages like English has prompted intense debate among cultural preservationists and economic pragmatists. While some observe that linguistic standardization streamlines international commerce and diplomacy, I firmly believe that governments bear a solemn moral obligation to finance the preservation of endangered indigenous languages, as each tongue encodes irreplaceable cultural and ecological wisdom.\\n\\nOn the one hand, proponents of linguistic convergence argue that a streamlined linguistic landscape fosters cross-cultural harmony and economic efficiency. In an era of interconnected global supply chains and multinational corporations, sharing a dominant lingua franca substantially mitigates communication barriers, reduces translation expenditures, and facilitates academic collaboration across borders. When developing nations prioritize instructional fluency in dominant world languages, their workforces can integrate seamlessly into global service sectors, such as information technology and international tourism, accelerating economic development. From a strictly utilitarian standpoint, linguistic uniformity appears to foster administrative simplicity and global synergy.\\n\\nOn the other hand, reducing human languages purely to tools of commercial exchange reflects a profound misunderstanding of human heritage. A language is not merely an arbitrary set of grammatical rules; it is a repository of a community's historical identity, ethical worldview, and ancestral wisdom. Many indigenous languages, such as those spoken by Amazonian or Arctic communities, possess intricate taxonomies for local medicinal flora and ecological phenomena that have no direct equivalents in modern languages. When a language becomes extinct, centuries of empirical botanical insight and cultural identity vanish irreversibly. State investments in bilingual education, oral history digitization, and indigenous literature curricula are modest expenses compared to the catastrophic loss of human cultural diversity.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while embracing international languages is pragmatic for global connectivity, this should never necessitate the sacrifice of indigenous tongues. Governments must actively invest in revitalizing endangered linguistic heritage, ensuring that economic globalization does not result in sterile cultural homogenization.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Richly nuanced philosophical and practical discussion. Exemplary vocabulary ('vehicular languages', 'intricate taxonomies', 'sterile cultural homogenization').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Balanced consideration of both perspectives\",\n      \"Clear, well-reasoned personal opinion\",\n      \"Sophisticated linguistic and ecological examples\",\n      \"Flawless sentence transitions\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-23\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Childhood Obesity and Sedentary Lifestyles\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Childhood obesity has reached unprecedented levels in many developed and developing countries, leading to serious long-term health complications.\\n\\nWhat are the primary factors contributing to this epidemic, and what practical measures can schools and governments take to reverse it?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase issue & outline essay\",\n        \"tips\": \"Highlight health crisis; point to ultra-processed foods and sedentary screentime as causes, followed by policy solutions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Primary causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Dietary shifts & physical inactivity\",\n        \"tips\": \"Aggressive marketing of sugary snacks, fast food, and hours spent on gaming or social media.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Actionable solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Government regulation & school reform\",\n        \"tips\": \"Sugar taxes, banning junk food ads to children, mandatory daily physical education in schools.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of measures\",\n        \"tips\": \"Multi-stakeholder approach combining legislative deterrence with educational physical activity.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The alarming escalation in pediatric obesity rates across both industrialized and emerging economies poses one of the most perilous public health challenges of the twenty-first century. This distressing trend is predominantly driven by the saturation of ultra-processed dietary products and the pervasive entrenchment of sedentary digital pastimes. Addressing this epidemic requires coordinated legislative interventions from public authorities and structural curriculum reforms within educational institutions.\\n\\nThe primary culprit behind escalating childhood obesity is the omnipresence of energy-dense, nutritionally deficient convenience foods. Modern multinational food corporations aggressively market confectionery, carbonated beverages, and fast food directly to impressionable minors through animated advertisements and digital influencer promotions. These items are engineered with hyper-palatable formulations of refined sugar, trans fats, and sodium that disrupt natural satiety signals. Exacerbating this caloric excess is the radical contraction of physical activity, as contemporary children frequently exchange outdoor athletics for sedentary hours spent playing video games or scrolling smartphones, drastically depressing daily energy expenditure.\\n\\nTo reverse this perilous trajectory, governments must implement stringent regulatory measures targeting the commercial food environment. A proven policy intervention is the imposition of statutory excise duties—such as sugar-sweetened beverage taxes—which compels manufacturers to reformulate products while elevating prices to deter consumption. Furthermore, regulatory agencies should outlaw the broadcasting of junk food advertisements during children's peak television and online viewing hours. Concurrently, schools must revamp their daily routines by instituting mandatory, non-negotiable physical education and providing subsidized, freshly prepared canteen meals that emphasize whole grains, fresh vegetables, and lean proteins.\\n\\nIn summary, childhood obesity is catalyzed by predatory food marketing and screen-centered sedentary lifestyles. Only through bold fiscal taxation on unhealthy foodstuffs and structured physical education in schools can society protect the vitality and longevity of future generations.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Strong task response with clear causal analysis and distinct, practical remedies. Advanced medical and nutritional vocabulary ('pediatric obesity', 'hyper-palatable formulations', 'caloric excess').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Distinct causes identified in Body 1\",\n      \"Specific, practical solutions divided between government and schools in Body 2\",\n      \"Accurate formal grammar\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-24\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Artificial Intelligence in Medical Diagnosis\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In healthcare, artificial intelligence algorithms are increasingly used to analyze medical scans, diagnose illnesses, and recommend treatment plans.\\n\\nDo the advantages of relying on artificial intelligence in medical diagnosis outweigh the disadvantages?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase topic & state clear verdict\",\n        \"tips\": \"State firmly that analytical precision and speed outweigh risks when AI operates as an assistive diagnostic tool.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Disadvantages & risks\",\n        \"focus\": \"Algorithmic bias, lack of clinical empathy, liability\",\n        \"tips\": \"Black-box decisions, potential errors on rare pathologies, absence of bedside bedside bedside rapport.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Speed, pattern recognition, expanding healthcare access\",\n        \"tips\": \"Analyzing thousands of MRI/X-rays in seconds, detecting early-stage oncological lesions, serving rural areas.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Definitive conclusion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Advantages clearly dominate because AI augments human doctors rather than replacing them entirely.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The integration of artificial intelligence into clinical diagnostics represents an epochal leap in modern healthcare, enabling automated neural networks to interpret radiological scans, sequence genomic data, and formulate therapeutic strategies. Although this automation raises valid concerns regarding algorithmic accountability and depersonalized patient care, I am convinced that the immense diagnostic accuracy, rapid processing capacity, and democratization of medical expertise far outweigh the drawbacks.\\n\\nCritics legitimately draw attention to the ethical and operational hazards inherent in algorithmic healthcare. The foremost dilemma is the 'black-box' nature of deep-learning algorithms, where neural networks reach diagnostic conclusions through opaque statistical weights that human physicians cannot easily audit. If an algorithmic tool delivers an erroneous diagnosis on an anomalous pathology, attributing medical malpractice becomes legally nebulous. Furthermore, algorithms trained on racially or socioeconomically skewed datasets risk perpetuating diagnostic disparities among minority patient demographics. Finally, automated systems inherently lack the emotional empathy, contextual nuance, and bedside manner that human practitioners supply when delivering devastating medical prognoses.\\n\\nNevertheless, the therapeutic benefits provided by artificial intelligence are extraordinary. Machine-learning models can scrutinize tens of thousands of radiographic images, such as mammograms and CT scans, in a fraction of a second, detecting microscopic oncological lesions and arterial anomalies years before they become perceptible to the human eye. In overburdened public health sectors and remote rural clinics that suffer acute shortages of specialized radiologists, cloud-based AI tools can provide instant, life-saving triage at negligible cost. By handling repetitive diagnostic screening, AI liberates clinicians to focus their time and energy on direct patient interaction and intricate surgical procedures.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while the ethical and accountability dilemmas of medical AI warrant stringent regulatory certification, its diagnostic precision and ability to deliver early disease detection are unmatched. As long as AI functions as a collaborative diagnostic aid overseen by human clinicians, its advantages unequivocally surpass its disadvantages.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Thoughtful weighing of pros and cons with clear medical terminology ('oncological lesions', 'therapeutic strategies', 'opaque statistical weights'). Cohesion is effortless.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explicitly resolved whether advantages outweigh disadvantages\",\n      \"Detailed medical examples used\",\n      \"Sophisticated paragraph transitions\",\n      \"Strong final synthesis\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-25\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Megacities and Rural Depopulation\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many parts of the world, people are migrating from rural villages to giant megacities in search of work and modern amenities. This leads to the depopulation of the countryside and overcrowding in urban centers.\\n\\nWhat problems does this trend create for both rural areas and cities?\\nWhat measures can be taken to establish a healthier balance between urban and rural development?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase urbanization issue & overview\",\n        \"tips\": \"State that the trend produces rural economic stagnation and urban strain; outline infrastructural decentralization solutions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Problems for both regions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Rural brain drain & urban infrastructural collapse\",\n        \"tips\": \"Aging rural populations, agricultural labor loss; urban gridlock, housing inflation, sanitation strain.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Practical solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Regional investment & digital infrastructure\",\n        \"tips\": \"Incentivize businesses to locate in provincial towns, upgrade rural high-speed internet, subsidize farming.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of vision\",\n        \"tips\": \"Balanced territorial development relieves urban pressure while rejuvenating countryside.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The relentless demographic exodus from agrarian hinterlands to sprawling metropolitan centers is reshaping the human geography of both developing and developed nations. This unchecked urbanization triggers profound socioeconomic dilemmas for both sending and receiving regions. This essay will examine the twin crises generated by this trend before presenting actionable remedies to restore regional balance.\\n\\nFor rural regions, the primary casualty of mass emigration is demographic and economic atrophy. As ambitious youth and skilled laborers depart for metropolises, rural communities are left with rapidly aging populations, leading to the closure of local schools, healthcare clinics, and commercial services. Crucially, the depletion of the agricultural workforce imperils national food security and causes ancestral farmlands to fall fallow. Conversely, receiving megacities face severe infrastructural asphyxiation. The influx of millions of newcomers outpaces municipal capacity, precipitating soaring property valuations, traffic gridlock, overstretched sewage systems, and the proliferation of informal shantytowns marked by precarious sanitary conditions.\\n\\nTo establish a sustainable equilibrium, public authorities must adopt deliberate regional decentralization policies. First, governments should invest heavily in high-speed digital broadband and modern transport corridors connecting provincial towns to commercial hubs, enabling distributed knowledge industries to operate outside congested capitals. Second, policymakers should offer fiscal incentives, including corporate tax holidays and discounted industrial land, to enterprises that establish regional headquarters in non-metropolitan districts. Furthermore, revitalizing rural economies requires state subsidies for smart agriculture, agro-tourism, and decentralized tertiary education campuses, thereby providing rural youth with viable economic futures without necessitating migration.\\n\\nTo conclude, unchecked urban migration inflicts economic abandonment on the countryside while choking urban infrastructure. Rebalancing this demographic divide demands aggressive state investment in rural digital infrastructure and economic incentives that decentralize industrial opportunity across the entire national territory.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Excellent response to both prompt questions. Strong contrasting analysis between rural and urban challenges. Rich vocabulary ('infrastructural asphyxiation', 'demographic and economic atrophy').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Covered problems for BOTH rural and urban zones in Body 1\",\n      \"Proposed concrete policy solutions in Body 2\",\n      \"Well-proportioned paragraphs\",\n      \"Accurate complex syntax\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-26\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Impact of Mass Tourism on Heritage Sites\",\n    \"promptText\": \"International mass tourism has grown rapidly, bringing revenue to local economies but often causing irreversible damage to ancient cultural monuments and pristine natural reserves.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree that governments should strictly limit the number of tourists permitted to visit fragile historical and natural heritage sites?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase tension & state position\",\n        \"tips\": \"Agree strongly that capping visitor quotas is necessary to preserve irreplaceable global heritage for posterity.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Physical degradation of sites\",\n        \"focus\": \"Foot traffic, microclimatic changes, erosion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Machu Picchu, Venice, coral reefs suffer structural damage and pollution from excessive crowds.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Sustainable tourism model\",\n        \"focus\": \"High-value, low-impact tourism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Permit systems and entry fees can maintain revenue while drastically reducing physical erosion.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Reiterate stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Short-term tourism profits must not compromise the eternal survival of cultural treasures.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The exponential expansion of commercial aviation and global travel has made once-remote historical sanctuaries and delicate ecosystems accessible to millions of holidaymakers. While this tourism boom injects vital foreign exchange into local economies, I completely agree that governments must enforce stringent visitor quotas on fragile heritage sites, as the physical preservation of irreplaceable cultural and natural treasures must take precedence over short-term commercial gain.\\n\\nThe unmitigated influx of tourists inevitably inflicts irreversible physical and environmental degradation on fragile sites. Ancient architectural marvels—such as the stone citadels of Machu Picchu, the historic canals of Venice, or ancient Egyptian subterranean tombs—were never engineered to withstand the daily footfalls, vibrations, and humidity changes generated by tens of thousands of visitors. In enclosed heritage chambers, the collective breath and perspiration of dense crowds alter microclimatic humidity levels, accelerating the flaking of irreplaceable ancient frescoes. Similarly, in delicate marine reserves like the Great Barrier Reef, excessive boat traffic, sunscreen chemical run-off, and anchor damage devastate marine ecosystems faster than they can naturally regenerate.\\n\\nMoreover, instituting statutory visitor limits does not inherently diminish tourism revenue if paired with a high-value, low-volume management model. By requiring advance reservations and charging substantial conservation tariffs, authorities can generate ample funds directly earmarked for restoration and site security while keeping physical attendance within sustainable carrying capacities. Nations like Bhutan have long practiced this philosophy with great success, levying a daily sustainable development fee that preserves unique Himalayan ecosystems and indigenous traditions while generating reliable state revenue.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the unconstrained commercial exploitation of ancient monuments and fragile ecologies constitutes cultural and environmental vandalism. Enforcing strict visitor quotas through advance ticketing is an essential stewardship measure that ensures humanity's finest treasures endure for future generations.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Fully developed argument with outstanding vocabulary ('unmitigated influx', 'carrying capacities', 'cultural and environmental vandalism'). Excellent Bhutanese case study.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Firm agreement maintained throughout\",\n      \"Vivid real-world examples cited\",\n      \"Addressed economic counterarguments convincingly\",\n      \"Error-free grammar and cohesion\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-27\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Mandatory Retirement Age vs Freedom to Work\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that governments should enforce a compulsory retirement age to free up jobs for younger generations. Others argue that older citizens should have the right to continue working for as long as they are willing and capable.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce debate & state thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge youth employment concerns, but argue mandatory retirement is age discrimination and wastes valuable expertise.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case for mandatory retirement\",\n        \"focus\": \"Youth unemployment, career stagnation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Young graduates need career progression; retirement creates vacancies in leadership.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Case for freedom to work\",\n        \"focus\": \"Human rights, economic longevity, institutional wisdom\",\n        \"tips\": \"Older workers possess decades of problem-solving skills; forced retirement causes financial distress and mental decline.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis & conclusion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Performance, not arbitrary age thresholds, should determine employment continuation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"With demographic shifts elevating life expectancy across the globe, the legal framework governing retirement has emerged as a controversial socioeconomic issue. While some contend that imposing a statutory retirement age is vital to stimulate career vacancies for young entrants, I firmly believe that capable older citizens should retain the uninhibited right to work, as arbitrary age discrimination damages both individual dignity and economic productivity.\\n\\nOn the one hand, advocates of compulsory retirement argue from the perspective of intergenerational mobility and youth employment. In fiercely competitive corporate and public-sector hierarchies, senior leadership positions are heavily occupied by older professionals. If senior executives and administrators remain in their posts indefinitely, upward promotion ladders become congested, leaving talented young graduates trapped in precarious, entry-level employment. Furthermore, proponents suggest that younger workers, having grown up in the digital era, bring cutting-edge technological competencies and innovative methodologies that conservative older managers may resist. From this viewpoint, mandatory retirement functions as a renewal mechanism that rejuvenates organizational dynamism.\\n\\nOn the other hand, compelling individuals to retire purely based on an arbitrary chronological age is fundamentally unjust and economically wasteful. Modern healthcare has extended both physical vitality and cognitive sharpness well beyond traditional pension milestones. Older employees possess invaluable institutional memory, emotional maturity, and complex dispute-resolution acumen that require decades of professional experience to cultivate. Forcing seasoned engineers, physicians, and educators out of the labor pool squanders invaluable human capital and exacerbates state pension deficits. Moreover, for many elderly citizens, active employment provides essential financial solvency, social engagement, and cognitive purpose; depriving them of this right often leads to psychological isolation and accelerated cognitive decline.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while concerns regarding youth career opportunities are legitimate, they should not be resolved through coercive generational displacement. Because human capability does not terminate at a predefined chronological threshold, employment should be governed by merit and competence rather than compulsory retirement ages.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Sophisticated discussion balancing intergenerational equity with human rights and economic reality. Elegant phrasing ('coercive generational displacement', 'institutional memory').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Thoroughly examined both perspectives\",\n      \"Clear, unwavering personal thesis\",\n      \"Balanced paragraphs with natural transitions\",\n      \"Wide lexical range\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-28\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Ocean Plastic Pollution and Marine Ecosystems\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Millions of tons of non-biodegradable plastic enter the world's oceans every year, threatening marine life, contaminating seafood food chains, and damaging global ecologies.\\n\\nWhat are the primary causes of this crisis, and what international solutions can be adopted to combat marine plastic pollution?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase plastic emergency & outline\",\n        \"tips\": \"Highlight non-biodegradable waste; identify single-use plastics and inadequate coastal waste systems; suggest international treaties and circular packaging.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Root causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Single-use consumerism, inadequate waste infrastructure\",\n        \"tips\": \"Excessive consumer packaging, rivers dumping mismanaged waste from developing regions into oceans.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: International solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Global legally binding treaty & innovative alternatives\",\n        \"tips\": \"UN treaty banning non-recyclable polymers, funding waste management in coastal nations, developing seaweed bioplastics.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of call to action\",\n        \"tips\": \"International regulatory alignment is mandatory for a transnational crisis.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The relentless accumulation of synthetic plastic debris in marine environments has escalated into an existential ecological catastrophe, jeopardizing oceanic biodiversity and threatening human food security through microplastic bioaccumulation. This crisis is fueled by the ubiquitous reliance on single-use packaging and substandard municipal waste infrastructure in riverine corridors. Resolving this crisis necessitates concerted, legally binding international treaties and substantial investment in circular materials science.\\n\\nThe principal cause of oceanic contamination is the unchecked proliferation of single-use petroleum polymers within the global consumer goods sector. From beverage bottles and grocery bags to synthetic food packaging, plastic is engineered for permanence yet utilized for minutes before being discarded. Because synthetic plastics do not biodegrade, they fragment into microscopic particles that absorb toxic pollutants and are ingested by marine fauna, infiltrating the human trophic chain. Compounding this issue is the stark inadequacy of waste-management systems in rapidly industrializing coastal nations. Major river systems—such as the Yangtze, Ganges, and Indus—act as aquatic conveyor belts, funneling vast quantities of mismanaged municipal trash straight into international waters.\\n\\nCombating this transboundary dilemma requires aggressive multilateral diplomacy and infrastructural intervention. First, the international community, through the United Nations, must enact a legally binding global plastics treaty that mandates the gradual phase-out of unnecessary single-use polymers and levies taxes on virgin plastic production. Second, affluent nations should establish a multilateral ecological fund to finance modern waste capture, sorting, and recycling infrastructure along the world's most polluting river deltas. Concurrently, public research grants should accelerate the commercialization of fully marine-biodegradable bioplastics derived from seaweed and agricultural waste, offering functional alternatives that decompose harmlessly in seawater.\\n\\nIn conclusion, marine plastic pollution is an insidious byproduct of disposable consumerism and deficient waste containment. Overcoming this global emergency demands binding international prohibitions on virgin single-use plastics alongside generous multilateral financing for waste management in vulnerable coastal regions.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Lucid problem-solution breakdown with precise ecological and scientific terminology ('microplastic bioaccumulation', 'human trophic chain', 'transboundary dilemma').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Distinct causes identified in Body 1\",\n      \"International solutions detailed in Body 2\",\n      \"Accurate use of transition markers\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-29\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Autonomous Self-Driving Vehicles\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Technological companies and automotive manufacturers are rapidly developing autonomous (self-driving) vehicles to replace human drivers on public roads.\\n\\nDo the advantages of widespread autonomous vehicles outweigh the disadvantages?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase driverless cars & thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"State clearly that despite technical and employment disruption, road safety and traffic optimization make advantages prevail.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Disadvantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Job displacement, cybersecurity, moral dilemmas\",\n        \"tips\": \"Taxi/truck drivers losing livelihood, software glitches, ethical algorithms in unavoidable accidents.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Drastic crash reduction, accessibility, traffic flow\",\n        \"tips\": \"Eliminating human error (drunk/distracted driving), mobility for elderly/disabled, coordinated fleet efficiency.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Definitive weighing\",\n        \"tips\": \"Safety and societal benefits far surpass transitional disruptions.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The development of autonomous vehicular technology represents a transformative frontier in personal transportation, promising to supplant human drivers with machine-vision sensors and predictive algorithmic navigation. Although this transition will undoubtedly induce widespread occupational dislocation and complex liability debates, I am convinced that the profound improvements in road safety, traffic efficiency, and universal mobility render the advantages substantially greater than the disadvantages.\\n\\nUndeniably, the mass deployment of driverless vehicles poses severe economic and ethical dilemmas. The most immediate fallout is the displacement of millions of professional commercial operators, including taxi drivers, bus operators, and long-haul truckers, whose livelihoods could be rendered obsolete within a decade. Reintegrating these displaced workers into knowledge-based industries will require extensive and costly state retraining programs. Additionally, autonomous transport networks present cybersecurity vulnerabilities; malicious hackers could potentially breach vehicle networks to create synchronized urban chaos. Furthermore, programming autonomous systems to navigate moral dilemmas during unavoidable collisions—such as deciding whether to protect vehicle occupants or pedestrians—poses profound legal and ethical quandaries.\\n\\nNevertheless, the safety and societal dividends of autonomous transit are overwhelmingly persuasive. The overwhelming majority of vehicular fatalities and collisions worldwide are directly attributable to human error, including intoxicated driving, speeding, sleep deprivation, and mobile phone distraction. Autonomous computer algorithms, by contrast, possess 360-degree LiDAR vision, instantaneous braking reaction times, and absolute immunity to emotional road rage or fatigue. Eliminating human fallibility from road transit will save tens of thousands of human lives annually. Moreover, vehicle-to-vehicle wireless communication will allow autonomous fleets to travel at optimized following distances, effectively smoothing traffic congestion and curtailing fuel emissions, while providing autonomous mobility to elderly citizens and individuals with physical disabilities.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while the displacement of commercial drivers and cybersecurity risks represent pressing challenges that require social safety nets, they are surpassed by the life-saving potential of autonomous transit. By dramatically reducing traffic fatalities and optimizing urban mobility, autonomous vehicles offer benefits that decisively outweigh their drawbacks.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Perfectly fulfills the essay type by explicitly weighing pros against cons. Employs advanced technical and vehicular lexicon ('vehicle-to-vehicle wireless communication', 'occupational dislocation').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explicitly answered the question of whether advantages outweigh disadvantages\",\n      \"Equally developed body paragraphs\",\n      \"Cohesive devices used naturally\",\n      \"No spelling or grammar errors\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-30\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Public Art and Statues in Cities\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Many cities spend significant amounts of public money on statues, sculptures, and outdoor art installations in parks and public squares.\\n\\nWhy do municipal governments invest in public art?\\nDo you consider this a worthwhile expenditure of taxpayers' money?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase topic & signpost\",\n        \"tips\": \"Explain that cities invest in art for civic identity and tourism; affirm that it is a worthwhile investment enriching civic life.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Why governments invest\",\n        \"focus\": \"Cultural identity, civic pride, tourism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Public art commemorates historical milestones, defines city branding, and attracts domestic/international visitors.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Is it worthwhile?\",\n        \"focus\": \"Mental well-being, social cohesion vs pure utility\",\n        \"tips\": \"Public art elevates psychological quality of life and democratizes culture beyond elitist private galleries.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of justification\",\n        \"tips\": \"Cities need both utilitarian infrastructure and artistic soul to truly thrive.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"Municipal authorities across the globe consistently channel substantial portions of municipal budgets into commissioning outdoor sculptures, monumental statues, and interactive public art installations. This essay will examine the primary motivators behind municipal investment in public art before demonstrating why such expenditure represents a thoroughly justifiable use of public funds.\\n\\nMunicipal governments allocate public capital to artistic installations primarily to cultivate civic identity and stimulate local economic prosperity through cultural tourism. Monuments and public sculptures often commemorate seminal historical figures, struggles for social justice, or unique cultural folklore, providing citizens with a tangible link to their shared heritage and instilling a sense of collective pride. On an economic level, iconic public installations—such as Chicago's Cloud Gate or Singapore's futuristic Supertree Grove—serve as powerful marketing landmarks that draw millions of international visitors, stimulating local hospitality, dining, and retail sectors.\\n\\nI firmly consider public expenditure on civic art to be a worthwhile and essential investment of taxpayers' funds. While critics frequently claim that artistic spending is an unwarranted luxury compared to utilitarian infrastructure like roads and hospitals, this perspective overlooks the profound psychological impact of the built environment on human well-being. Monotonous, utilitarian urban landscapes dominated purely by concrete and commercial billboards foster alienation and mental fatigue. Incorporating aesthetic beauty into public squares democratizes cultural access, ensuring that inspiring artistic works can be appreciated freely by all citizens, regardless of socioeconomic standing, rather than remaining the exclusive preserve of wealthy patrons in private galleries.\\n\\nIn conclusion, municipal governments commission public art to anchor collective civic heritage and stimulate tourism. Because public art democratizes cultural enrichment and revitalizes urban mental well-being, allocating public revenue toward aesthetic civic enhancements is entirely justified.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Directly answers both questions in distinct paragraphs. Elegant argument defending the societal role of aesthetics beyond pure utility. Flawless syntax and vocabulary.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Addressed reasons for investment in Body 1\",\n      \"Addressed whether it is worthwhile in Body 2\",\n      \"Well-supported personal stance\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-31\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"Funding Professional Athletes vs Grassroots Sports\",\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people argue that governments should invest millions of dollars into training elite professional athletes to win medals at international competitions like the Olympics. Others believe that this money should be directed toward building community sports facilities for ordinary citizens.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree that community sports should take priority over elite athletics funding?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase debate & clear stance\",\n        \"tips\": \"Agree strongly that grassroots community facilities must take fiscal precedence over elite athletic sponsorships.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Public health benefits of community facilities\",\n        \"focus\": \"Combating lifestyle diseases, mental well-being\",\n        \"tips\": \"Local swimming pools, football pitches, and community gyms directly lower national healthcare expenditures.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Flaws of elite investment priority\",\n        \"focus\": \"Narrow elite beneficiaries vs broad social inclusion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Winning Olympic medals brings fleeting national pride but leaves ordinary citizens sedentary and unfit.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate position\",\n        \"tips\": \"A healthy, active general population is a far more meaningful measure of national success than a handful of gold medals.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The strategic disbursement of public sports funding often presents national governments with a dilemma between prestige and public health. While some champion substantial expenditures on elite athletic academies to attain glory at international tournaments like the Olympic Games, I wholeheartedly agree that public funding should prioritize the development of grassroots community sports facilities for everyday citizens, as public health and social inclusion far outweigh fleeting national prestige.\\n\\nFirst and foremost, allocating public funds to community sports infrastructure directly addresses the burgeoning crisis of sedentary lifestyle diseases. Modern societies are experiencing unprecedented rates of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, and mental anxiety. Providing subsidized, easily accessible public swimming pools, cycling tracks, and neighborhood sports halls empowers ordinary families and school-children to incorporate regular exercise into their daily routines. The long-term return on this investment is immense, as an active population drastically relieves financial pressure on overburdened national healthcare systems.\\n\\nConversely, disproportionate investment in elite athletic programs represents an inefficient use of public capital that benefits only a minuscule fraction of the population. Pouring millions of dollars into bespoke altitude training camps, biomechanical laboratories, and high-performance coaches for a handful of Olympic hopefuls may generate temporary patriotic euphoria during a fortnight of televised sporting broadcasts. However, empirical studies reveal that elite medal success produces negligible 'trickle-down' increases in actual grassroots physical participation. On the contrary, when funds are diverted to specialized velodromes while local municipal leisure centers are shuttered due to budget cuts, ordinary citizens are deprived of foundational recreational opportunities.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while celebrating elite sporting excellence is commendable, the fundamental duty of government is to safeguard the welfare of the collective public. Prioritizing accessible grassroots sports facilities over elite medal chasing yields enduring public health dividends that are infinitely more valuable to society.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Powerful, well-structured argument that rejects the counter-argument with statistical logic. Sophisticated academic vocabulary ('patriotic euphoria', 'intergenerational physical dividends').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Uncompromising opinion clearly sustained\",\n      \"Comparative analysis between elite and grassroots funding\",\n      \"Cohesive paragraph development\",\n      \"Rich vocabulary\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-32\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Standardized Exams vs Continuous Assessment\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In many education systems, students' academic success is measured primarily through final standardized examinations. Other educators argue that continuous assessment through coursework, presentations, and group projects provides a fairer evaluation of student ability.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce evaluation debate & opinion\",\n        \"tips\": \"Acknowledge objectivity of exams, but argue continuous assessment offers a far more authentic reflection of real-world ability.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: In defense of standardized examinations\",\n        \"focus\": \"Objectivity, anti-plagiarism, benchmark standard\",\n        \"tips\": \"Exams minimize teacher bias, prevent cheating, and provide uniform standards across different schools.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: In defense of continuous assessment\",\n        \"focus\": \"Comprehensive skill evaluation, reduced anxiety\",\n        \"tips\": \"Coursework evaluates research, teamwork, and prolonged effort rather than memorization under timed stress.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis & hybrid recommendation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Continuous evaluation should form the cornerstone of academic grading, complemented by moderated exams.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The methodology by which academic competence is evaluated represents one of the most enduring debates in modern pedagogy. While traditionalists argue that high-stakes standardized examinations provide an objective, impartial benchmark of student attainment, I believe that continuous assessment through diverse coursework and practical projects offers a far more authentic and comprehensive measure of a student's true capabilities.\\n\\nOn the one hand, proponents of standardized examinations emphasize their rigorous impartiality and uniformity. In a terminal written exam conducted under strict invigilation, every student confronts identical questions under uniform time constraints, virtually eliminating subjective grading bias from familiar classroom teachers. Standardized exams also provide a robust deterrent against plagiarism and unauthorized external assistance, which can easily taint coursework completed outside supervised school settings. Furthermore, for universities and employers assessing thousands of applicants, standardized scores offer a convenient, universally recognized metric to benchmark candidates from wildly disparate educational backgrounds.\\n\\nOn the other hand, relying exclusively on high-stakes terminal examinations is fundamentally flawed and pedagogically archaic. A single three-hour examination often assesses rote memorization and performance under acute psychological stress rather than deep conceptual understanding. Students who experience severe test anxiety or temporary illness may perform disastrously despite possessing intellectual brilliance. Conversely, continuous assessment—incorporating independent research essays, oral presentations, laboratory experiments, and collaborative group tasks—evaluates the holistic competencies required in modern professional workplaces, such as critical problem-solving, project management, and collaborative communication over sustained time horizons.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while standardized examinations offer administrative consistency and fraud prevention, they are too reductive to encapsulate human intellect accurately. Educational systems should adopt continuous assessment as the primary evaluative mechanism, ensuring that diligence, creativity, and practical problem-solving are rewarded fairly.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Outstanding comparison of educational assessment models. Precise pedagogical terms ('pedagogically archaic', 'invigilation', 'terminal examinations').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Addressed both testing approaches fairly\",\n      \"Clear personal opinion stated and reinforced\",\n      \"Exemplary syntactic complexity\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-33\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"Antibiotic Resistance in Healthcare and Agriculture\",\n    \"promptText\": \"The growing resistance of infectious bacteria to common antibiotics has become a major global health security threat.\\n\\nWhat are the primary factors causing antibiotic resistance, and what coordinated international measures should be taken to prevent a global health catastrophe?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase antimicrobial resistance & outline\",\n        \"tips\": \"Highlight post-antibiotic threat; identify medical overprescription and livestock abuse as causes; outline regulatory and scientific solutions.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Primary causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Human overprescription & agricultural misuse\",\n        \"tips\": \"Doctors prescribing antibiotics for viral colds; industrial farming feeding livestock prophylactic antibiotics to promote growth.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Coordinated solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Stricter prescription laws, banning farm antibiotics, R&D\",\n        \"tips\": \"Outlaw over-the-counter sales, ban subtherapeutic livestock antibiotics, subsidize novel pharmaceutical antibiotic research.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of measures\",\n        \"tips\": \"Multilateral coordination across human and animal health ('One Health' approach).\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The emergence of multidrug-resistant bacteria, often colloquially referred to as 'superbugs', constitutes one of the gravest existential threats to modern medical science. Without effective antimicrobial agents, routine surgeries and minor infections could once again become fatal. This crisis is predominantly driven by the reckless overprescription of antibiotics in human healthcare and their indiscriminate prophylactic use in livestock agriculture. Averting this catastrophe requires strict statutory pharmaceutical regulation and international research cooperation.\\n\\nThe genesis of antimicrobial resistance lies in the pervasive misuse and overuse of antibiotic compounds. In many jurisdictions, antibiotics are sold over-the-counter without a physician's prescription, leading individuals to self-medicate for viral conditions such as the common cold, against which antibiotics are biologically useless. Even when properly prescribed, patients frequently terminate their medication regimens prematurely once symptoms subside, allowing surviving mutant bacteria to adapt and propagate resistance. Even more alarming is the agricultural sector, where massive factory farms routinely mix subtherapeutic doses of antibiotics into animal feed, not to cure sickness, but to artificially accelerate livestock growth and prevent infections in overcrowded feedlots.\\n\\nTo counter this looming catastrophe, sovereign governments must institute stringent oversight through a coordinated 'One Health' international framework. First, regulatory authorities must strictly classify antibiotics as prescription-only pharmaceuticals, penalizing pharmacies that dispense them without certified clinical diagnostic testing. Second, international bodies like the World Health Organization must enforce an immediate, worldwide prohibition on the use of medically important antibiotics for livestock growth promotion. Concurrently, governments should offer lucrative market incentives and direct subsidies to pharmaceutical companies to invest in the research and development of novel classes of antimicrobial drugs and alternative phage therapies.\\n\\nIn conclusion, antimicrobial resistance is a man-made crisis fueled by careless medical prescribing and agricultural exploitation. Preserving the efficacy of these life-saving medicines demands uncompromising international bans on non-essential antibiotic usage and aggressive public funding for next-generation pharmacological discovery.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Exemplary cause-and-solution structure. Features accurate medical and microbiological terminology ('multidrug-resistant bacteria', 'prophylactic use', 'phage therapies').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Distinct causes (healthcare + agriculture) identified\",\n      \"Specific international interventions detailed\",\n      \"Consistent formal register\",\n      \"Well within time/word limits\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-34\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Urban Vertical Farming and Controlled Agriculture\",\n    \"promptText\": \"As urban populations expand and arable land diminishes, scientists have developed vertical indoor farming, where crops are grown in stacked layers using artificial light and controlled nutrient water systems.\\n\\nDo the advantages of urban vertical farming outweigh the disadvantages?\\n\\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase vertical farming & state thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"State clearly that despite high energy costs, land conservation and year-round food security make advantages dominate.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Disadvantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"High energy consumption, limited crop variety\",\n        \"tips\": \"Intensive electricity required for LED lighting; high capital setup costs; unable to grow staple calorie crops like wheat or rice easily.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Water efficiency, no pesticides, localized supply chains\",\n        \"tips\": \"Uses 95% less water, eliminates agricultural runoff, eliminates transport emissions by growing food inside cities.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesis & conclusion\",\n        \"tips\": \"As clean energy becomes cheaper, the advantages of vertical farming will overwhelmingly triumph.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The acute pressure on planetary soil reserves caused by climate volatility and demographic expansion has spurred the emergence of vertical indoor farming—a controlled-environment agricultural technique wherein crops are cultivated in vertically stacked shelves utilizing LED illumination and hydroponic or aeroponic nutrient systems. While this agricultural innovation currently grapples with steep energy inputs and limited crop diversity, I firmly believe that its environmental efficiencies, immunity to climatic hazards, and food security benefits decisively outweigh its drawbacks.\\n\\nAdmittedly, vertical farming entails significant operational limitations. The most prominent impediment is the exorbitant capital expenditure required to construct climate-controlled indoor facilities outfitted with precision robotics, sensors, and continuous artificial lighting. Because indoor farms rely entirely on artificial luminescence rather than free natural sunlight, their electricity consumption is substantial, which could exacerbate greenhouse gas emissions if powered by fossil-fuel grids. Furthermore, current vertical technology is commercially viable primarily for leafy greens, culinary herbs, and berries; it remains largely unfeasible for calorie-dense staple crops like wheat, rice, and potatoes, which nourish the bulk of humanity.\\n\\nNevertheless, the environmental and logistical advantages of vertical agriculture are groundbreaking. Traditional open-field agriculture accounts for roughly seventy percent of global freshwater withdrawals and causes catastrophic ecological harm via synthetic pesticide and fertilizer runoff. In stark contrast, closed-loop vertical systems recycle up to ninety-five percent of water inputs and operate in hermetically sealed environments that eliminate the need for chemical herbicides and pesticides. Furthermore, because vertical farms can be established inside abandoned urban warehouses, produce can be harvested and delivered to urban consumers within hours, drastically shortening food supply chains and reducing transport refrigeration emissions.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while high electricity requirements and crop limitations currently restrict vertical farming, these hurdles will diminish as renewable energy and lighting efficiencies advance. Because vertical farming conserves vast quantities of water, restores depleted wild ecosystems, and guarantees food security independent of weather droughts, its advantages overwhelmingly outstrip its disadvantages.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.5. Outstanding task fulfillment with clear weighing of pros and cons. Superior agronomic vocabulary ('controlled-environment agricultural technique', 'hydroponic or aeroponic nutrient systems').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Explicitly answered whether advantages outweigh disadvantages\",\n      \"Equally developed body paragraphs\",\n      \"High-level vocabulary and natural collocation\",\n      \"Over 250 words\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-35\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"two_part\",\n    \"title\": \"Celebrity Culture and Its Influence on Teenagers\",\n    \"promptText\": \"In modern society, many young people idolize celebrities such as social media influencers, reality television personalities, and pop musicians, obsessively tracking their lifestyles.\\n\\nWhat are the reasons for this intense fascination with celebrities?\\nIs this a positive or negative influence on young people?\",\n    \"minWordCount\": 250,\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase phenomenon & signpost\",\n        \"tips\": \"Explain that digital accessibility drives fascination; argue that the overall influence is predominantly detrimental to youth development.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Reasons for fascination\",\n        \"focus\": \"Social media intimacy, escapism, identity search\",\n        \"tips\": \"Curated behind-the-scenes content creates parasocial relationships; teenagers seek aspirational role models during identity formation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Positive or negative?\",\n        \"focus\": \"Predominantly negative: unrealistic standards & materialism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Distorted body image, glorification of unearned wealth, consumerist envy, and superficial career goals.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of verdict\",\n        \"tips\": \"Celebrity adoration fosters psychological insecurity and shallow materialistic values among vulnerable youth.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"In the contemporary digital landscape, the obsession with celebrity lifestyles—ranging from Hollywood actors and pop icons to social media influencers—has attained an unprecedented prominence among teenagers. This essay will examine the psychological mechanisms underpinning this fascination before arguing that this cultural phenomenon exerts a predominantly deleterious influence on adolescent development.\\n\\nThe pervasive fascination with famous personalities is fueled primarily by the intimate architecture of modern social media and the developmental psychology of adolescence. Unlike past generations who viewed celebrities through distant television screens, contemporary teenagers interact with influencers through platforms like Instagram and TikTok, where celebrities broadcast real-time, behind-the-scenes glimpses into their private routines. This continuous accessibility fosters 'parasocial relationships'—one-sided psychological bonds where adolescents feel deeply acquainted with the celebrity. Furthermore, during adolescence, individuals naturally seek external templates to anchor their nascent identities and aspirations, making glamourous public figures natural magnets for teenage emulation.\\n\\nI contend that this obsession is profoundly detrimental to the emotional and intellectual well-being of young people. The primary hazard is the promotion of unattainable lifestyle and beauty standards. Influencers routinely present heavily filtered, digitally retouched aesthetics, which fosters chronic body dissatisfaction, low self-esteem, and mental health crises among impressionable teenagers. Moreover, celebrity culture frequently glorifies instant wealth, conspicuous consumerism, and superficial notoriety over foundational virtues such as academic diligence, empathy, and vocational mastery. When adolescents conflate authentic accomplishment with social media follower counts, they develop distorted worldviews that prize superficial vanity over substantive personal development.\\n\\nIn conclusion, teenagers idolize celebrities due to the illusion of digital intimacy and their instinctive search for identity. However, because this culture normalizes unattainable physical perfection and materialistic consumerism, its overarching impact on youth is unmistakably harmful.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Band 8.0. Thorough answer to both questions with incisive psychological reasoning. Advanced vocabulary ('parasocial relationships', 'nascent identities', 'conspicuous consumerism').\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"checklist\": [\n      \"Answered Question 1 (causes) in Body 1\",\n      \"Answered Question 2 (evaluation) in Body 2\",\n      \"Firm, justified negative verdict\",\n      \"Strong cohesion and grammar\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-36\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"opinion\",\n    \"title\": \"University Tuition Fees Should Be Abolished\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n    \"topic\": \"education\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 6.5,\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people believe that university education should be completely free for all citizens, funded entirely by the government. Others argue that students should pay for their higher education because they benefit individually through higher salaries.\\n\\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree with the proposal to make university tuition completely free?\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase & clear thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Agree that free tuition fosters social mobility, while acknowledging progressive taxation needed.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Economic and Social Mobility\",\n        \"focus\": \"Democratizing education\",\n        \"tips\": \"Removes debt barriers for working-class youth, expands skilled workforce.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Societal Return on Investment\",\n        \"focus\": \"Public good\",\n        \"tips\": \"Graduates pay higher lifetime taxes, driving technological innovation and civic health.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restate thesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Free tuition is a strategic societal investment rather than a private luxury.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The debate over higher education financing is a contentious socio-economic issue in contemporary policy. While critics contend that individual students should bear the financial burden of their degrees due to enhanced personal earning potential, I firmly believe that tertiary education should be fully subsidized by the state to drive equitable meritocracy and national productivity.\\n\\nFirst and foremost, eliminating tuition fees dismantles deep-seated socio-economic barriers that prevent talented individuals from disadvantaged backgrounds from accessing higher learning. When degrees carry exorbitant costs, lower-income students are either completely deterred from enrolling or are forced to graduate with crippling loan debts that stifle early-career entrepreneurship, homeownership, and geographical mobility. In contrast, tuition-free tertiary systems—such as those operating in Scandinavia and Germany—ensure that admission is governed strictly by academic aptitude rather than parental wealth, unleashing latent talent across all strata of society.\\n\\nFurthermore, universal tertiary education yields immense positive externalities that benefit society as a whole rather than merely the individual degree holder. Modern knowledge economies depend critically on high concentrations of skilled engineers, medical doctors, researchers, and educators to remain globally competitive. Highly educated workers generate accelerated technological innovation, elevate public health outcomes, and contribute vastly higher sums in progressive income taxes over their lifetimes, effectively reimbursing the state treasury for their educational subsidies. Regarding higher education as a commodified private consumer product undermines the foundational principle that an educated populace is a collective public good.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although funding universal higher education requires substantial public fiscal expenditure, the long-term societal, civic, and economic returns vastly outweigh the initial costs. Governments should abolish tertiary tuition to foster genuine social equality and economic dynamism.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Response: Fully addresses all aspects of the prompt with well-developed ideas and clear position throughout. Coherence & Cohesion: Logically organized with sophisticated discourse markers. Lexical Resource: Wide range of academic vocabulary used with precision. Grammatical Range & Accuracy: Full range of complex syntactic structures with complete flexibility.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"minWordCount\": 250\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-37\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"discussion\",\n    \"title\": \"Preserving Traditional Architecture vs Modern Glass High-Rises\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n    \"topic\": \"culture\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 7.5,\n    \"promptText\": \"Some people argue that modern cities should preserve their historic buildings and traditional architectural styles. Others believe that older structures should be demolished to build modern skyscrapers and efficient infrastructure.\\n\\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce both sides\",\n        \"tips\": \"State opinion: historic preservation and modern density can be symbiotically integrated.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Case for modern development\",\n        \"focus\": \"Space efficiency, modern insulation\",\n        \"tips\": \"Skyscrapers house thousands and use less energy than drafty old ruins.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Value of heritage preservation\",\n        \"focus\": \"Cultural identity & tourism\",\n        \"tips\": \"Historic architecture gives cities soul, continuity, and immense tourist revenue.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion & Opinion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Adaptive reuse synthesis\",\n        \"tips\": \"Heritage facade preservation combined with modern interior retrofitting offers the optimal balance.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The rapid urbanization of modern metropolises frequently pits the preservation of architectural heritage against the acute demand for high-density modern housing and infrastructure. While proponents of modernization emphasize land efficiency and economic progress, advocates of heritage argue that historic structures are indispensable anchors of cultural identity. In my view, urban policy should champion adaptive reuse, preserving historic facades while modernizing structural interiors.\\n\\nOn the one hand, advocates of demolition and high-rise construction present compelling utilitarian arguments. Rapid demographic expansion in global cities generates catastrophic housing shortages and commercial rent inflation. Traditional two-story masonry buildings utilize urban land inefficiently, whereas contemporary high-rise towers can accommodate thousands of residents and offices on an identical geographic footprint. Moreover, modern architectural designs incorporate superior earthquake resilience, centralized high-efficiency HVAC systems, and accessibility ramps that historical edifices simply cannot support without prohibitive modification costs.\\n\\nOn the other hand, the cultural and psychological value of historic architecture cannot be quantified in purely financial terms. Historic buildings embody a society's collective memory, craftsmanship, and aesthetic heritage, transforming generic concrete urban canyons into distinctive, livable communities. Demolishing century-old townhouses in favor of uniform glass and steel monoliths erodes civic pride and homogenization of global cities. Furthermore, preserved heritage districts serve as powerful economic drivers, attracting international cultural tourism that enriches local retail, dining, and artistic economies.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while growing cities unquestionably require high-density modern infrastructure, obliterating historic landmarks is a short-sighted mistake. City authorities should adopt sympathetic hybrid policies—preserving exterior heritage architecture while thoroughly modernizing interior infrastructure.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Response: Exceptionally well-balanced examination of both perspectives with insightful critical analysis. Coherence & Cohesion: Skilfully orchestrated progression across paragraphs. Lexical Resource: Natural and nuanced academic collocations. Grammatical Range & Accuracy: Error-free execution of varied complex clauses and passive constructions.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"minWordCount\": 250\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-38\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"problem_solution\",\n    \"title\": \"The Growing Epidemic of Sedentary Lifestyles and Obesity\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n    \"topic\": \"health\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 6.5,\n    \"promptText\": \"In many countries around the world, adult and childhood obesity rates are escalating rapidly due to sedentary screen habits and the prevalence of ultra-processed fast food.\\n\\nWhat are the primary causes of this public health crisis, and what effective measures can governments and communities take to tackle it?\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Paraphrase problem\",\n        \"tips\": \"Mention desk-bound work, ubiquitous smartphones, and cheap hyper-palatable processed food.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Root causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Environment & economics\",\n        \"tips\": \"Sedentary entertainment combined with aggressive junk food marketing.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Solutions\",\n        \"focus\": \"Policy & civic action\",\n        \"tips\": \"Sugar taxes, subsidized community sports, mandatory school cooking and nutrition classes.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Summary of measures\",\n        \"tips\": \"Multi-pronged strategy combining legislative regulation with accessible recreation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8,\n        \"text\": \"The exponential escalation of obesity rates among both adults and children represents one of the most formidable public health challenges of the twenty-first century. Rooted in pervasive digital sedentary behaviors and the hyper-availability of nutrient-poor fast food, this crisis threatens to overwhelm public healthcare infrastructure. However, decisive legislative taxation and community physical design interventions can reverse this trend.\\n\\nThe principal drivers of escalating obesity are twofold: occupational and technological sedentarism, combined with an industrialized food supply. Modern office employment and school learning are almost exclusively desk-bound, while leisure hours are increasingly dominated by screen-based entertainment like video games and social media algorithms, sharply reducing daily caloric expenditure. Concurrently, commercial food corporations heavily market ultra-processed foods saturated with refined sugars, hydrogenated vegetable oils, and synthetic emulsifiers. These hyper-palatable foods are commercially engineered to bypass natural satiety cues, while their artificially low retail prices make them the most accessible sustenance for time-poor, low-income families.\\n\\nTo remediate this escalating crisis, governments must enact rigorous public health regulations. First, authorities should introduce targeted fiscal measures, such as excise taxes on sugar-sweetened beverages and ultra-processed confectionery, utilizing the accrued tax revenues to subsidize fresh organic fruits and vegetables in low-income neighborhoods. Furthermore, legislation must ban junk-food advertising across children's digital media and television networks. At the civic level, municipalities must invest aggressively in active urban infrastructure—constructing protected cycling corridors, public outdoor fitness parks, and mandating daily compulsory physical education and nutritious hot meals in primary and secondary schools.\\n\\nIn conclusion, obesity is not merely a failure of individual willpower, but a systemic societal byproduct of modern sedentary living and toxic food environments. Implementing targeted food taxes alongside expanded public recreational facilities is essential to safeguard public health.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Response: Evaluates economic and cultural arguments thoroughly with an unequivocal stance. Coherence & Cohesion: Flawless cohesion and seamless topical transitions. Lexical Resource: Sophisticated register with accurate academic terminology. Grammatical Range & Accuracy: Precise use of modal auxiliary verbs and subordinate clauses.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"minWordCount\": 250\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-39\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"advantages_disadvantages\",\n    \"title\": \"Remote Work and the Decoupling of Office and Home\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"medium\",\n    \"topic\": \"work\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 7,\n    \"promptText\": \"Since the global pandemic, an increasing number of companies have permitted employees to work from home on a permanent or hybrid basis.\\n\\nDo the advantages of remote working outweigh the disadvantages for both employers and employees?\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Introduce remote work phenomenon\",\n        \"tips\": \"Thesis: Benefits in flexibility, reduced commuting, and broader talent pools far outweigh challenges.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Disadvantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Isolation & communication hurdles\",\n        \"tips\": \"Blurring of work-life boundaries, lack of serendipitous collaboration, loneliness.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Advantages\",\n        \"focus\": \"Autonomy, productivity, cost savings\",\n        \"tips\": \"Hours saved on commuting, lower corporate real estate overhead, access to global talent.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Weighing balance\",\n        \"tips\": \"With robust digital tools and intentional hybrid check-ins, advantages clearly dominate.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The widespread adoption of remote and hybrid working arrangements over recent years has catalyzed a paradigm shift in modern employment dynamics. While telecommuting introduces genuine challenges regarding social cohesion and work-life balance, I am convinced that its substantial advantages—in enhanced personal autonomy, eliminated commuter fatigue, and expanded organizational productivity—markedly outweigh the drawbacks.\\n\\nAdmittedly, working remotely is not without legitimate operational pitfalls. Chief among these is the erosion of clear psychological boundaries between professional duties and private domestic life. When the living room becomes the workplace, employees often find it difficult to disconnect, leading to digital fatigue, unpaid overtime, and eventual burnout. Furthermore, remote environments can diminish spontaneous collaborative brainstorming—the creative serendipity that occurs when colleagues converse informally around office spaces. For new recruits and junior trainees, the absence of in-person mentorship and informal peer observation can impede professional development and foster feelings of professional isolation.\\n\\nNevertheless, the multifaceted benefits of remote employment for both workforce and management are overwhelming. For employees, reclaiming the hours previously squandered in stressful, expensive daily commutes directly translates into superior physical health, better domestic relationships, and reduced transportation expenditures. Empirical productivity analyses demonstrate that when spared distracting open-plan office chatter, knowledge workers execute deep analytical tasks with heightened focus and fewer interruptions. For employers, embracing remote work eliminates massive commercial real estate leasing overheads and utility costs. Crucially, it empowers corporations to recruit elite talent from across the globe, rather than restricting their talent search to individuals living within commuting distance of a corporate headquarters.\\n\\nIn conclusion, although remote work requires deliberate managerial adaptations to maintain team culture, its capacity to enhance worker quality of life and optimize corporate efficiency ensures that its merits far surpass its disadvantages.\",\n        \"examinerCommentary\": \"Task Response: Directly explores causes and pragmatic solutions with real-world examples. Coherence & Cohesion: Clear thematic organization with transparent paragraph signposting. Lexical Resource: Rich topic-specific lexical items applied naturally. Grammatical Range & Accuracy: Wide variety of compound-complex sentences executed with consistent accuracy.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"recommendedMinutes\": 40,\n    \"minWordCount\": 250\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"t2-40\",\n    \"taskType\": \"task2\",\n    \"essayType\": \"double_question\",\n    \"title\": \"Youth Crime Rates and the Role of Rehabilitation vs Retribution\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"hard\",\n    \"topic\": \"crime\",\n    \"estimatedBand\": 8,\n    \"promptText\": \"In many urban areas, rates of petty and violent crime committed by adolescents are on the rise.\\n\\nWhy is youth crime increasing in modern cities, and what is the most effective approach to handle juvenile offenders?\",\n    \"planningGuide\": [\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Introduction\",\n        \"focus\": \"Acknowledge issue\",\n        \"tips\": \"State causes (family breakdown, lack of youth opportunities, social media influence) and approach (restorative rehabilitation).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 1: Causes\",\n        \"focus\": \"Socio-economic factors\",\n        \"tips\": \"Marginalization, peer pressure, lack of mental health resources.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Body 2: Best approach\",\n        \"focus\": \"Restorative justice & vocational training\",\n        \"tips\": \"Incarceration breeds hardened criminals; community mentoring and education reduce recidivism.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"paragraph\": \"Conclusion\",\n        \"focus\": \"Synthesize\",\n        \"tips\": \"Address root socio-economic triggers with compassionate, structured rehabilitation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"sampleAnswers\": [\n      {\n        \"band\": 8.5,\n        \"text\": \"The escalating prevalence of juvenile delinquency across modern urban centers is an alarming sociological phenomenon that poses severe threats to civic cohesion. Rooted in systemic socio-economic deprivation, familial instability, and the proliferation of antisocial digital subcultures, this crisis cannot be resolved through retributive incarceration alone. Rather, societies must implement restorative rehabilitation and holistic educational intervention.\\n\\nThe genesis of surging youth criminality lies in a complex interplay of socio-economic and psychological vulnerabilities. In economically marginalized urban precincts, under-resourced public schools and the chronic absence of community youth facilities leave adolescents with vast unmonitored leisure hours. Compounding this void is the breakdown of traditional domestic support structures; parents working multiple low-wage jobs often lack the time to provide essential psychological oversight. Disillusioned adolescents frequently seek belonging, identity, and financial remuneration in street gangs. Furthermore, modern social media platforms amplify this delinquency by glorifying gang culture, cyber-bullying, and illicit drug dealing, accelerating the recruitment of impressionable teenagers into criminal behavior.\\n\\nRegarding the handling of juvenile offenders, punitive custodial sentences are demonstrably counterproductive. Housing young offenders in conventional correctional facilities exposes them to seasoned adult criminals, institutionalizing antisocial attitudes and virtually guaranteeing adult recidivism. Instead, judicial systems must champion restorative justice and vocational rehabilitation. Non-violent offenders should be diverted into mandatory community service programs where they directly repair the damage inflicted on their communities. Simultaneously, the state must pair offenders with professional youth social workers and enroll them in apprenticeships in software development, carpentry, or electrical trades. Equipping troubled adolescents with marketable skills and emotional counseling addresses the fundamental causes of their delinquency.\\n\\nIn conclusion, yout